WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.773 --> 00:00:13.202
- It's my pleasure to welcome
you to our new facility.

00:00:13.202 --> 00:00:16.787
We're, as you gathered, pretty
proud of our space here.

00:00:16.787 --> 00:00:17.743
I'm Bobbie Pilette,

00:00:17.743 --> 00:00:20.142
I'm director of preservation department

00:00:20.142 --> 00:00:22.581
and for the Center of
Preservation and Conservation

00:00:22.581 --> 00:00:25.686
for Yale University libraries.

00:00:25.686 --> 00:00:29.647
Welcome to our 7th of our
preservation lectures.

00:00:29.647 --> 00:00:32.544
This one is sponsored
by Jack, class of '47,

00:00:32.544 --> 00:00:34.425
and Betsy O'Neill.

00:00:34.425 --> 00:00:36.224
As you will hear from our panel,

00:00:36.224 --> 00:00:38.241
conservation needs are evolving.

00:00:38.241 --> 00:00:41.697
And to meet those needs,
the spaces are changing.

00:00:41.697 --> 00:00:44.391
Though for some of you who saw our space

00:00:44.391 --> 00:00:46.798
with some of the equipment
that probably looks familiar

00:00:46.798 --> 00:00:49.786
from 15th-century
prints, you're wondering,

00:00:49.786 --> 00:00:51.406
"Is it really new?"

00:00:51.406 --> 00:00:53.489
Yes, there's lots of new.

00:00:55.103 --> 00:00:57.855
Today's conservation labs
deal not only with the old,

00:00:57.855 --> 00:01:00.179
but also needs to take into consideration

00:01:00.179 --> 00:01:02.723
the new demands and
expanded use of science

00:01:02.723 --> 00:01:05.656
and study of cultural materials.

00:01:05.656 --> 00:01:07.313
Our panelists have all been involved

00:01:07.313 --> 00:01:08.870
in renovations of a space

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:12.292
or a complete design of new spaces.

00:01:12.292 --> 00:01:14.180
Full disclosure,

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:16.717
it wasn't until we had assembled the panel

00:01:16.717 --> 00:01:19.297
that I realized that all of our panelists

00:01:19.297 --> 00:01:22.423
are graduates of the
University of Texas at Austin

00:01:22.423 --> 00:01:24.729
library and information
sciences master's program,

00:01:24.729 --> 00:01:25.816
(laughter)

00:01:25.816 --> 00:01:27.858
and have a certificate in advanced study

00:01:27.858 --> 00:01:31.257
in library and archival conservation.

00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:33.995
But as the only graduate-level
conservation training program

00:01:33.995 --> 00:01:36.306
that focused on libraries and archives,

00:01:36.306 --> 00:01:38.639
it's perhaps not a surprise.

00:01:39.618 --> 00:01:42.398
So let me introduce our panelists.

00:01:42.398 --> 00:01:43.539
I'm going to introduce them

00:01:43.539 --> 00:01:45.368
in the order they'll be speaking.

00:01:45.368 --> 00:01:48.575
Beth Doyle, who's head of
conservation services department

00:01:48.575 --> 00:01:51.402
and the Leona B. Carpenter
senior conservator

00:01:51.402 --> 00:01:53.607
at Duke University libraries

00:01:53.607 --> 00:01:55.977
where she's been since 2002.

00:01:55.977 --> 00:01:57.963
She holds a bachelor's
of arts and photography

00:01:57.963 --> 00:01:59.543
from the University of Dayton,

00:01:59.543 --> 00:02:02.837
and a master's and advanced
certificate from UT.

00:02:02.837 --> 00:02:05.614
She's active at the local and state level,

00:02:05.614 --> 00:02:08.340
and has served on several
American Library Associations,

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:12.073
and American Institute for
Conservation interest groups.

00:02:12.073 --> 00:02:14.188
Beth was granted fellow status

00:02:14.188 --> 00:02:18.506
in the American Institute
for Conservation in 2014.

00:02:18.506 --> 00:02:22.091
Our next speaker is Jennifer Hain Teper.

00:02:22.091 --> 00:02:23.558
She's the Bud, Veld?

00:02:23.558 --> 00:02:24.391
- Velde.

00:02:24.391 --> 00:02:26.488
- Velde preservation librarian,

00:02:26.488 --> 00:02:27.626
head of preservation

00:02:27.626 --> 00:02:31.640
at the University of Illinois libraries.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:34.724
She began there as head
of conservation in 2001.

00:02:34.724 --> 00:02:37.407
She had gotten her master's
and advanced certificate

00:02:37.407 --> 00:02:39.526
from UT in 2000.

00:02:39.526 --> 00:02:40.359
She now teaches

00:02:40.359 --> 00:02:42.737
several preservation and
conservation-related courses

00:02:42.737 --> 00:02:45.188
in the graduate school of
library and information science

00:02:45.188 --> 00:02:47.280
at the University of Illinois,

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:48.879
and also serves as an instructor

00:02:48.879 --> 00:02:52.429
at the Campbell Center
for Historic Preservation.

00:02:52.429 --> 00:02:54.985
She designed the University
of Illinois's conservation lab

00:02:54.985 --> 00:02:57.037
which opened in 2006,

00:02:57.037 --> 00:02:59.714
and edited the 2012 book

00:02:59.714 --> 00:03:01.205
The Planning and Construction

00:03:01.205 --> 00:03:06.177
of Book and Paper Conservation
Laboratories: A Guidebook.

00:03:06.177 --> 00:03:08.527
Our third speaker is Eliza Gilligan.

00:03:08.527 --> 00:03:09.705
She's the book conservator

00:03:09.705 --> 00:03:11.556
for the university library collections

00:03:11.556 --> 00:03:13.960
at the University of Virginia library,

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:18.191
and she's worked there
since March of 2009.

00:03:18.191 --> 00:03:19.328
Prior to UVA,

00:03:19.328 --> 00:03:22.231
Eliza worked at the Smithsonian
Institution libraries

00:03:22.231 --> 00:03:24.859
and Michigan State University libraries.

00:03:24.859 --> 00:03:27.568
Eliza designed labs for
all three institutions,

00:03:27.568 --> 00:03:30.092
and participated in multiple renovations

00:03:30.092 --> 00:03:32.460
and reconfigurations of conservation

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.682
and preservation workspaces.

00:03:34.682 --> 00:03:37.931
She received her master's
and advanced certificate

00:03:37.931 --> 00:03:39.264
from UT in 2000.

00:03:40.551 --> 00:03:42.837
And finally there's
our Christine McCarthy.

00:03:42.837 --> 00:03:44.205
She is the chief conservator

00:03:44.205 --> 00:03:46.626
for the Yale University library
preservation department's

00:03:46.626 --> 00:03:49.806
conservation and exhibition services.

00:03:49.806 --> 00:03:51.809
She joined the staff in 2008

00:03:51.809 --> 00:03:54.445
as the head of the special
collections collection.

00:03:54.445 --> 00:03:57.701
In 2010, Christine's
responsibilities expanded

00:03:57.701 --> 00:03:59.890
to include the conservation operations

00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:02.151
for the general collections.

00:04:02.151 --> 00:04:03.845
In addition to exhibition support

00:04:03.845 --> 00:04:06.720
and special collections treatment.

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:09.256
She represents Yale University's
conservation committee

00:04:09.256 --> 00:04:10.776
as an active member

00:04:10.776 --> 00:04:15.367
of Mellon-funded international
project ConservationSpace.

00:04:15.367 --> 00:04:17.629
Christine received her master's
and advanced certificate

00:04:17.629 --> 00:04:22.038
from UT, and has a BFA in
illustration and design

00:04:22.038 --> 00:04:24.825
from the University of
the Arts in Philadelphia.

00:04:24.825 --> 00:04:27.325
She's a professional associate

00:04:30.227 --> 00:04:33.130
member of the American
Institute for Conservation.

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:34.920
In her previous positions

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:37.424
at the University of Chicago and MIT

00:04:37.424 --> 00:04:39.435
she established new programs.

00:04:39.435 --> 00:04:41.791
At the University of Chicago
she worked with architects

00:04:41.791 --> 00:04:43.757
to design a treatment laboratory

00:04:43.757 --> 00:04:45.359
as part of the building expansion

00:04:45.359 --> 00:04:48.293
for the Regenstein library.

00:04:48.293 --> 00:04:49.369
The way we're gonna structure this

00:04:49.369 --> 00:04:53.200
is each of the panelists have
a brief formal presentation,

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:55.401
and then I'll pose a series of questions

00:04:55.401 --> 00:04:58.597
that they will start to address.

00:04:58.597 --> 00:05:00.824
What we're hoping to do
is start a conversation.

00:05:00.824 --> 00:05:02.743
So we'll go through those
series of questions.

00:05:02.743 --> 00:05:04.852
You each have an index card,

00:05:04.852 --> 00:05:08.144
there's a pencil floating
around somewhere in your row.

00:05:08.144 --> 00:05:10.236
Feel free to jot down questions.

00:05:10.236 --> 00:05:12.369
We are recording this

00:05:12.369 --> 00:05:15.417
and the microphones you
see are for the recording,

00:05:15.417 --> 00:05:17.071
we're not amplifying anything.

00:05:17.071 --> 00:05:20.375
We also have an overflow room
where there's some folks,

00:05:20.375 --> 00:05:22.472
and so they will be the ones that will

00:05:22.472 --> 00:05:24.873
have to rely solely on the index cards.

00:05:24.873 --> 00:05:26.431
We'll have somebody bringing them in.

00:05:26.431 --> 00:05:27.488
But for those of you here,

00:05:27.488 --> 00:05:30.309
you are free to ask the question yourself.

00:05:30.309 --> 00:05:31.557
But if you don't want to

00:05:31.557 --> 00:05:34.176
you can pass the index card
to the center of the aisle

00:05:34.176 --> 00:05:36.581
when I call for that and
someone will take it up

00:05:36.581 --> 00:05:40.191
and I'll simply relay the
question to the panelists.

00:05:40.191 --> 00:05:43.506
So please join me in
welcoming our panelists,

00:05:43.506 --> 00:05:45.987
and Beth will be the first one to speak.

00:05:45.987 --> 00:05:48.237
(applause)

00:05:51.811 --> 00:05:53.228
- Good afternoon.

00:05:54.102 --> 00:05:55.329
Oh, so in the South, I should say,

00:05:55.329 --> 00:05:57.491
that you have to respond
to that or it's very rude.

00:05:57.491 --> 00:06:01.664
Good afternoon.
(crowd responds and laughs)

00:06:01.664 --> 00:06:04.997
I learned that lesson really quickly.

00:06:04.997 --> 00:06:07.814
So I wanna thank Yale and
Bobbie for inviting me today,

00:06:07.814 --> 00:06:08.872
and thank you all for coming.

00:06:08.872 --> 00:06:09.934
This is a beautiful space

00:06:09.934 --> 00:06:12.576
and we appreciate you sharing it with us.

00:06:12.576 --> 00:06:13.915
The Duke University library's

00:06:13.915 --> 00:06:16.433
Vern and Tanya Roberts Conservation Lab

00:06:16.433 --> 00:06:18.037
is a hybrid lab,

00:06:18.037 --> 00:06:19.818
that is we work on both
special collections

00:06:19.818 --> 00:06:23.901
as well as general
collections in the same space.

00:06:25.454 --> 00:06:28.480
I was hired in 2002 to develop
the conservation program.

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:32.405
At the time we had three
technicians and myself.

00:06:32.405 --> 00:06:36.677
Our space consisted of two
large rooms that you see here,

00:06:36.677 --> 00:06:39.533
separated by a semi-public hallway,

00:06:39.533 --> 00:06:43.701
and we shared both of these
rooms with the Bindery Unit.

00:06:43.701 --> 00:06:46.659
Our supplies were in that
hallway on pallets on the floor

00:06:46.659 --> 00:06:48.774
and on hand-me-down shelving.

00:06:48.774 --> 00:06:51.366
My staff was really productive

00:06:51.366 --> 00:06:54.516
despite the fact that
they spent a lot of time

00:06:54.516 --> 00:06:56.827
and physical energy working
around the limitations

00:06:56.827 --> 00:06:58.330
of that space.

00:06:58.330 --> 00:06:59.479
We had no room for growth,

00:06:59.479 --> 00:07:03.615
we didn't have space to
add a lot of equipment,

00:07:03.615 --> 00:07:06.384
and this really limited
the types of treatments

00:07:06.384 --> 00:07:07.217
that we could do,

00:07:07.217 --> 00:07:09.759
especially for special collections.

00:07:09.759 --> 00:07:12.545
The good news was that the
library had already embarked

00:07:12.545 --> 00:07:14.838
on a major renovation

00:07:14.838 --> 00:07:18.103
that included a new conservation lab.

00:07:18.103 --> 00:07:22.270
We moved to temporary space
on the medical campus in 2006,

00:07:23.814 --> 00:07:26.731
and the new lab was opened in 2008.

00:07:29.519 --> 00:07:32.069
The new lab was purpose-built for us.

00:07:32.069 --> 00:07:36.206
We have adequate space to move
around and store supplies.

00:07:36.206 --> 00:07:40.373
And we were also able to
install a lot of large equipment

00:07:43.056 --> 00:07:46.615
as well as things like fume
hoods and washing sinks.

00:07:46.615 --> 00:07:49.041
Duke University is also very committed

00:07:49.041 --> 00:07:51.509
to having good ergonomic workspaces,

00:07:51.509 --> 00:07:53.864
and because of that we have a cork floor,

00:07:53.864 --> 00:07:56.898
electric sit-to-stand
benches for everybody,

00:07:56.898 --> 00:08:00.481
and special ergonomic
chairs at each bench.

00:08:02.854 --> 00:08:06.634
The new space allowed us
to improve our workflows

00:08:06.634 --> 00:08:08.900
to better control our supply inventories,

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:12.011
which means better budget control, right?

00:08:12.011 --> 00:08:16.040
And improve our documentation practices.

00:08:16.040 --> 00:08:18.028
We also have room to add staff

00:08:18.028 --> 00:08:21.792
or spread out if a treatment
demands that space.

00:08:21.792 --> 00:08:23.829
Most importantly, it
allowed us to envision

00:08:23.829 --> 00:08:26.596
a more robust and integrated
conservation program

00:08:26.596 --> 00:08:27.894
for the library.

00:08:27.894 --> 00:08:31.525
We were able to expand our
services to include mold removal,

00:08:31.525 --> 00:08:33.321
chemical treatments, washing,

00:08:33.321 --> 00:08:35.600
and humidification and flattening.

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:38.304
It also allowed us to
develop and expand services

00:08:38.304 --> 00:08:41.495
for the exhibits program and
the digital production center.

00:08:41.495 --> 00:08:44.303
And if you're interested to
see a video tour of the lab,

00:08:44.303 --> 00:08:47.303
there's one on YouTube at that link.

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:52.686
The greatest benefit of this space

00:08:52.686 --> 00:08:55.727
is the opportunities that
is affords our program.

00:08:55.727 --> 00:08:57.696
We recently hosted Gawain Weaver's

00:08:57.696 --> 00:09:01.450
photograph conservation for
non-photo conservators workshop,

00:09:01.450 --> 00:09:04.682
and we invited about 15
people plus the instructors

00:09:04.682 --> 00:09:06.716
into the space.

00:09:06.716 --> 00:09:08.389
We also hosted an object conservator

00:09:08.389 --> 00:09:10.646
who spent a few days working in our lab

00:09:10.646 --> 00:09:13.880
on items from our history
of medicine collection.

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:17.674
Having the flexibility to
have conservators come to us

00:09:17.674 --> 00:09:22.035
exposes us to other
conservation specialties,

00:09:22.035 --> 00:09:25.646
and builds our conservation community.

00:09:25.646 --> 00:09:26.479
I'm very interested

00:09:26.479 --> 00:09:28.511
in doing more of these sorts
of things in the future,

00:09:28.511 --> 00:09:31.889
and our space really allows us to do that.

00:09:31.889 --> 00:09:34.708
The lab has become a major stop

00:09:34.708 --> 00:09:38.675
on the library development office tour.

00:09:38.675 --> 00:09:41.171
We give tours to students and faculty,

00:09:41.171 --> 00:09:44.336
advisory board members, donors, alumni,

00:09:44.336 --> 00:09:47.511
and colleagues interested
in renovating their labs.

00:09:47.511 --> 00:09:49.751
Most importantly, our space and services

00:09:49.751 --> 00:09:51.793
are highlighted on tours for donors

00:09:51.793 --> 00:09:54.309
whose collections they
are considering depositing

00:09:54.309 --> 00:09:56.017
at our library.

00:09:56.017 --> 00:09:58.682
The work we do is a value-added service

00:09:58.682 --> 00:10:01.008
that we can provide for their collections,

00:10:01.008 --> 00:10:04.518
and shows our commitment to
the care of those materials.

00:10:04.518 --> 00:10:05.676
And last year,

00:10:05.676 --> 00:10:09.128
we received a donation to
conservation's adopt-a-book fund

00:10:09.128 --> 00:10:13.020
as a direct result of the
behind-the-scenes tours we hosted

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:14.520
on alumni weekend.

00:10:15.562 --> 00:10:18.289
So it was really exciting
to be part of the outreach

00:10:18.289 --> 00:10:21.170
and fundraising efforts of the library.

00:10:21.170 --> 00:10:24.388
I believe by engaging
our library colleagues,

00:10:24.388 --> 00:10:27.603
the academic community,
and donors in these ways

00:10:27.603 --> 00:10:30.272
we not only get to show
off our work and space,

00:10:30.272 --> 00:10:33.488
but we can demonstrate
that we are a key component

00:10:33.488 --> 00:10:35.448
in making collections accessible,

00:10:35.448 --> 00:10:39.941
and are an integral part
of the life of the library.

00:10:39.941 --> 00:10:41.324
Thank you.

00:10:41.324 --> 00:10:43.574
(applause)

00:10:47.649 --> 00:10:49.316
- Hi, thank you all.

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:51.393
- [Person In Audience] Hi.

00:10:51.393 --> 00:10:52.487
(laughter)

00:10:52.487 --> 00:10:56.957
- I'm from Pennsylvania, I
don't have the same requirement.

00:10:56.957 --> 00:11:00.898
I'm here representing the
University of Illinois's lab.

00:11:00.898 --> 00:11:03.699
I think I actually have the oldest lab.

00:11:03.699 --> 00:11:05.222
Our lab is now 10 years old.

00:11:05.222 --> 00:11:07.214
This is a picture from the good old days

00:11:07.214 --> 00:11:08.856
before we moved over.

00:11:08.856 --> 00:11:10.812
This is as nice as our old space got.

00:11:10.812 --> 00:11:12.957
I want to point out a
couple of highlights,

00:11:12.957 --> 00:11:16.505
other than our fantastic staff, of course.

00:11:16.505 --> 00:11:19.796
Our old space was, as many of you know,

00:11:19.796 --> 00:11:21.964
or have similar experiences,

00:11:21.964 --> 00:11:23.302
in the basement.

00:11:23.302 --> 00:11:25.495
We did have garden-level lighting

00:11:25.495 --> 00:11:28.310
which looked out onto the
very scenic loading dock

00:11:28.310 --> 00:11:31.589
which sucked in fumes periodically,
which was really nice.

00:11:31.589 --> 00:11:34.108
We had the most fantastic

00:11:34.108 --> 00:11:38.257
matching 1950's vintage
all-steel office furniture ever.

00:11:38.257 --> 00:11:41.510
Which was exceedingly
non-ergonomic (laughs)

00:11:41.510 --> 00:11:43.980
and really didn't offer
very good workspace.

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:46.534
But obviously we made it work.

00:11:46.534 --> 00:11:48.532
The biggest hindrance
here to our old space

00:11:48.532 --> 00:11:50.588
and the big reason for
pushing for a new lab

00:11:50.588 --> 00:11:51.788
was that there was absolutely no water

00:11:51.788 --> 00:11:53.573
or chemical treatments
possible in this space.

00:11:53.573 --> 00:11:55.646
It was just a basement
space in our library.

00:11:55.646 --> 00:11:59.480
And so our goal was to
get out of our space

00:11:59.480 --> 00:12:01.035
and off of that floor entirely

00:12:01.035 --> 00:12:02.980
before we clogged up the women's room sink

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:05.620
with so much PVA buildup
that everybody hated us.

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:06.945
Cuz there was only one sink

00:12:06.945 --> 00:12:08.515
to wash out all of our brushes in.

00:12:08.515 --> 00:12:11.682
And it was shared with the laboratory.

00:12:12.917 --> 00:12:14.335
Good news is that when I came

00:12:14.335 --> 00:12:17.476
to the University of Illinois in 2001,

00:12:17.476 --> 00:12:18.454
there was hope.

00:12:18.454 --> 00:12:20.031
(laughter)

00:12:20.031 --> 00:12:22.164
The library had embarked on,

00:12:22.164 --> 00:12:23.585
like many other academic libraries,

00:12:23.585 --> 00:12:26.356
funding to build a Harvard-style

00:12:26.356 --> 00:12:27.972
high-density storage facility.

00:12:27.972 --> 00:12:31.340
And in that was planned
a conservation lab.

00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:32.588
So when they hired my position

00:12:32.588 --> 00:12:34.318
as the first head of conservation,

00:12:34.318 --> 00:12:35.591
the plan was to entice me

00:12:35.591 --> 00:12:38.841
with this forthcoming conservation lab.

00:12:39.755 --> 00:12:41.471
That was 2001.

00:12:41.471 --> 00:12:45.011
In 2003, when they actually
built and finished,

00:12:45.011 --> 00:12:46.706
well 2003-2004,

00:12:46.706 --> 00:12:47.760
finished the construction

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:49.124
on the high-density storage facility,

00:12:49.124 --> 00:12:50.707
they recognized that they had not budgeted

00:12:50.707 --> 00:12:54.039
nearly enough money for the
very hi-tech HVAC system

00:12:54.039 --> 00:12:56.794
that high-density book storage requires,

00:12:56.794 --> 00:12:58.611
and the only thing you can
actually cut out of that budget

00:12:58.611 --> 00:13:00.505
was the conservation lab,

00:13:00.505 --> 00:13:02.023
because everything else was book storage.

00:13:02.023 --> 00:13:04.777
So then there was a lot
of heavy fundraising

00:13:04.777 --> 00:13:06.189
to make the lab happen,

00:13:06.189 --> 00:13:08.826
which is why I'm now at the
Bud Velde Conservation Lab.

00:13:08.826 --> 00:13:12.036
Thank goodness for Bud Velde
at the University of Illinois.

00:13:12.036 --> 00:13:15.993
So we moved into that
space actually, in 2006.

00:13:15.993 --> 00:13:16.868
Simple floor plan,

00:13:16.868 --> 00:13:17.868
simply because our space

00:13:17.868 --> 00:13:19.321
doesn't photograph real
well, unfortunately.

00:13:19.321 --> 00:13:22.086
Lots of long, skinny spaces.

00:13:22.086 --> 00:13:23.191
But just to give you an overview

00:13:23.191 --> 00:13:24.505
of what we have in the space.

00:13:24.505 --> 00:13:27.762
Obviously a nice big lab
space, wet lab, et cetera

00:13:27.762 --> 00:13:29.338
I'll show you some individual pictures.

00:13:29.338 --> 00:13:31.834
Ample office space for staff growth,

00:13:31.834 --> 00:13:34.103
which we have almost
completely encompassed now

00:13:34.103 --> 00:13:35.203
within 10 years.

00:13:35.203 --> 00:13:37.470
I thought we had more space
than we could possibly use.

00:13:37.470 --> 00:13:38.680
After 10 years

00:13:38.680 --> 00:13:42.528
we filled up pretty much every
single niche in this lab.

00:13:42.528 --> 00:13:45.336
Dirty space, which I'll be
talking about in just a second.

00:13:45.336 --> 00:13:47.657
But most importantly, we have this giant

00:13:47.657 --> 00:13:51.824
conference room/reference
space/classroom/everything else.

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:53.872
And that has honestly been

00:13:53.872 --> 00:13:55.761
one of the most valuable spaces for us

00:13:55.761 --> 00:13:57.500
because we do a lot of education

00:13:57.500 --> 00:14:00.208
with our graduate school of
library and information science.

00:14:00.208 --> 00:14:02.648
We do a lot of donor
relations and bring people in

00:14:02.648 --> 00:14:04.736
and kind of do a lot of
orientations in there.

00:14:04.736 --> 00:14:07.113
But it's also great surge
space for us to have,

00:14:07.113 --> 00:14:09.807
you know, the random, huge,
moldy collections that come in

00:14:09.807 --> 00:14:11.539
that we need to put somewhere.

00:14:11.539 --> 00:14:14.564
It's great to have an extra
surge space built like that.

00:14:14.564 --> 00:14:16.093
I would say that this whole square footage

00:14:16.093 --> 00:14:17.757
is about 5,000 square feet.

00:14:17.757 --> 00:14:20.372
And I was really, really, really proud,

00:14:20.372 --> 00:14:21.235
I am still,

00:14:21.235 --> 00:14:22.537
very proud of our lab.

00:14:22.537 --> 00:14:23.948
But I have to say that coming here

00:14:23.948 --> 00:14:25.452
and seeing the beautiful
facilities at Yale

00:14:25.452 --> 00:14:27.503
has been maybe wah-wah-wah (laughs)

00:14:27.503 --> 00:14:28.644
(laughter)

00:14:28.644 --> 00:14:31.266
Illinois, uh. (laughs)

00:14:31.266 --> 00:14:33.466
But you guys still have the
new car smell on your lab,

00:14:33.466 --> 00:14:35.400
and mine's worn off, so.

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:36.762
Okay, so looking at the space,

00:14:36.762 --> 00:14:38.656
we have fantastic natural light.

00:14:38.656 --> 00:14:41.056
We actually may have too
much natural light now.

00:14:41.056 --> 00:14:42.824
Despite shades and whatnot,

00:14:42.824 --> 00:14:44.604
we do find that in the winter months,

00:14:44.604 --> 00:14:47.218
sometimes it still gets
a bit bright in our lab.

00:14:47.218 --> 00:14:49.105
But it's so much better
than being in a basement.

00:14:49.105 --> 00:14:51.705
The natural light is obviously fantastic.

00:14:51.705 --> 00:14:53.792
Almost nothing is fixed in our lab.

00:14:53.792 --> 00:14:55.093
Everything is mobile,

00:14:55.093 --> 00:14:57.467
which has turned out to be essential

00:14:57.467 --> 00:14:59.143
as our program has changed.

00:14:59.143 --> 00:15:00.609
And that's one of the things that I think,

00:15:00.609 --> 00:15:03.920
because I came in when there
almost really was no program

00:15:03.920 --> 00:15:05.339
at the University of Illinois.

00:15:05.339 --> 00:15:07.018
We were one of the first
labs that was funded

00:15:07.018 --> 00:15:09.274
as part of the Mellon
Foundation initiative

00:15:09.274 --> 00:15:10.272
to start building labs

00:15:10.272 --> 00:15:12.360
in institutions that didn't have them.

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:13.522
So when I came in,

00:15:13.522 --> 00:15:15.405
I was the head of conservation,

00:15:15.405 --> 00:15:17.418
and the only person in conservation

00:15:17.418 --> 00:15:19.472
other than one book-repair tech.

00:15:19.472 --> 00:15:23.139
And we've grown to six
FTE and 2.5 hourlies.

00:15:25.291 --> 00:15:27.334
2.5 FTE hourlies at this point.

00:15:27.334 --> 00:15:29.747
So planning for how those
people were gonna use that space

00:15:29.747 --> 00:15:30.733
was kind of challenging

00:15:30.733 --> 00:15:32.944
when you're designing a lab
with no staff there yet.

00:15:32.944 --> 00:15:35.372
And so the fluidity and the
mobility of all that equipment

00:15:35.372 --> 00:15:37.614
was really, really important.

00:15:37.614 --> 00:15:41.682
We've got one little wall of
built-in cabinets and whatnot.

00:15:41.682 --> 00:15:44.147
But absolutely everything
else is mobile and adjustable.

00:15:44.147 --> 00:15:46.644
And ergonomic and height-adjustable
and everything else.

00:15:46.644 --> 00:15:48.488
All those good things.

00:15:48.488 --> 00:15:50.893
We do have the essential
wet lab, obviously.

00:15:50.893 --> 00:15:53.080
This is one of the areas that

00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:55.669
I didn't plan very well for.

00:15:55.669 --> 00:15:57.506
I'm a book conservator,

00:15:57.506 --> 00:16:00.152
or was a book conservator,
I should say, unfortunately,

00:16:00.152 --> 00:16:01.697
and so when we designed this lab,

00:16:01.697 --> 00:16:03.696
I'm thinking "books."

00:16:03.696 --> 00:16:05.390
Then we hired a paper conservator.

00:16:05.390 --> 00:16:08.220
You paper conservators take up
a phenomenal amount of space.

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:09.451
Oh my god.

00:16:09.451 --> 00:16:11.526
So when our paper conservator came,

00:16:11.526 --> 00:16:14.469
we had to re-think not only
the layout of the general lab,

00:16:14.469 --> 00:16:15.972
which I said was mobile,
so we made it work,

00:16:15.972 --> 00:16:18.774
but the wet lab has now kind
of become her territory.

00:16:18.774 --> 00:16:22.974
And so what I though
ample, six-foot fume hood,

00:16:22.974 --> 00:16:26.154
is no longer ample for
our paper conservator.

00:16:26.154 --> 00:16:27.387
So now we have to look at

00:16:27.387 --> 00:16:29.729
we're actually actively
installing a snorkel system

00:16:29.729 --> 00:16:32.083
to make basically every
working surface in that space

00:16:32.083 --> 00:16:34.303
suitable for fume evacuation,

00:16:34.303 --> 00:16:37.435
which it was not, previously.

00:16:37.435 --> 00:16:39.114
And then of all the rooms

00:16:39.114 --> 00:16:40.708
that I wish we had more square footage,

00:16:40.708 --> 00:16:43.677
we have our dirty/quarantine space,

00:16:43.677 --> 00:16:45.067
which is really much more quarantine

00:16:45.067 --> 00:16:46.688
than it ever has been dirty.

00:16:46.688 --> 00:16:49.280
Although it gets dirty, but
in the really yucky sense.

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:50.744
This is where anything buggy,

00:16:50.744 --> 00:16:54.027
wet, pooped-on, moldy, et cetera,

00:16:54.027 --> 00:16:56.024
comes in and gets quarantined.

00:16:56.024 --> 00:16:57.146
And we have been shocked

00:16:57.146 --> 00:17:01.323
by the embrace from our
special collections units

00:17:01.323 --> 00:17:03.126
in having this space and bringing in

00:17:03.126 --> 00:17:04.502
all of their disgusting gifts

00:17:04.502 --> 00:17:07.826
into our space for us to deal with them.

00:17:07.826 --> 00:17:11.493
This looks really empty
and almost spacious.

00:17:13.005 --> 00:17:14.728
Usually every surface in here

00:17:14.728 --> 00:17:16.829
is covered with something, unfortunately.

00:17:16.829 --> 00:17:18.114
Because our buildings are very old

00:17:18.114 --> 00:17:19.013
they leak like a sieve,

00:17:19.013 --> 00:17:20.947
and we're always getting in gross gifts.

00:17:20.947 --> 00:17:24.864
So I wish I could triple
the size of this room.

00:17:25.750 --> 00:17:26.676
Alright.

00:17:26.676 --> 00:17:27.968
Thank you, and we'll move on.

00:17:27.968 --> 00:17:30.218
(applause)

00:17:33.221 --> 00:17:34.388
- Hi everyone.

00:17:36.131 --> 00:17:36.997
Let's see.

00:17:36.997 --> 00:17:39.329
The University of Virginia library.

00:17:39.329 --> 00:17:41.712
I am the first book conservator

00:17:41.712 --> 00:17:44.619
ever hired by the university library,

00:17:44.619 --> 00:17:46.646
which is kind of amazing

00:17:46.646 --> 00:17:50.729
considering the scope of
their rare book library.

00:17:52.126 --> 00:17:53.989
But the program was underway

00:17:53.989 --> 00:17:56.697
briefly before I got there.

00:17:56.697 --> 00:17:59.419
Holly Robertson was the
previous head of preservation.

00:17:59.419 --> 00:18:02.336
She had a nascent book repair unit.

00:18:03.300 --> 00:18:05.261
There was a commercial binding operation,

00:18:05.261 --> 00:18:07.460
and then she wrote a Mellon grant

00:18:07.460 --> 00:18:09.793
to sort of be like Jen said,

00:18:11.783 --> 00:18:14.807
start a conservation
lab, hire a conservator,

00:18:14.807 --> 00:18:16.355
and then create an endowment

00:18:16.355 --> 00:18:20.646
to fund the preservation
administrator position.

00:18:20.646 --> 00:18:25.116
So things happen, you write
a grant, you have a plan,

00:18:25.116 --> 00:18:27.343
you think it's just
gonna go like clockwork,

00:18:27.343 --> 00:18:29.467
and then things change.

00:18:29.467 --> 00:18:30.975
When Holly wrote the grant,

00:18:30.975 --> 00:18:32.818
she thought that the university

00:18:32.818 --> 00:18:35.476
was going to coalesce around this idea.

00:18:35.476 --> 00:18:39.170
She had brought on board
the university art museum,

00:18:39.170 --> 00:18:42.371
the office of the architect,
and the Kluge-Ruhe Art Museum,

00:18:42.371 --> 00:18:43.473
which is a huge collection

00:18:43.473 --> 00:18:47.385
of Australian Aboriginal bark paintings.

00:18:47.385 --> 00:18:51.065
And she had identified
a building on campus

00:18:51.065 --> 00:18:53.381
just a little bit apart
from the main campus

00:18:53.381 --> 00:18:54.214
that we could build a big

00:18:54.214 --> 00:18:57.575
collaborative conservation facility

00:18:57.575 --> 00:19:01.742
sort of something like this,
maybe a little smaller.

00:19:04.155 --> 00:19:06.443
Just a conservation facility.

00:19:06.443 --> 00:19:08.992
So she wrote the grant with that in mind,

00:19:08.992 --> 00:19:12.163
they hired me with that
in mind, the idea being,

00:19:12.163 --> 00:19:14.704
"This is all gonna happen,
this is all gonna happen,"

00:19:14.704 --> 00:19:18.641
so by the time I moved to Charlottesville

00:19:18.641 --> 00:19:21.663
and show up on my first day, they're like,

00:19:21.663 --> 00:19:23.994
"Well, things have changed a little bit.

00:19:23.994 --> 00:19:25.499
"We're still gonna do

00:19:25.499 --> 00:19:27.630
"the collaborative conservation facility,

00:19:27.630 --> 00:19:28.669
"but meanwhile we have

00:19:28.669 --> 00:19:31.277
"a temporary conservation space for you

00:19:31.277 --> 00:19:35.347
"in a swing space
building on campus, where,

00:19:35.347 --> 00:19:37.500
"when we're renovating the art studios

00:19:37.500 --> 00:19:39.417
"we put their offices."

00:19:40.470 --> 00:19:42.977
So they gave me this space,

00:19:42.977 --> 00:19:46.740
which was formerly a photography dark room

00:19:46.740 --> 00:19:48.453
for the art studio.

00:19:48.453 --> 00:19:51.973
And Holly's original plan
for this space had been,

00:19:51.973 --> 00:19:54.165
"Oh, they're gonna give
us 2400 square feet,

00:19:54.165 --> 00:19:55.586
"and we can put in book repair,

00:19:55.586 --> 00:19:57.650
"we can put in box-making,
we can put in supplies,"

00:19:57.650 --> 00:19:59.335
and this that and the other.

00:19:59.335 --> 00:20:00.285
The university said,

00:20:00.285 --> 00:20:01.118
"No, no, no.

00:20:01.118 --> 00:20:02.444
"You get this end of the building.

00:20:02.444 --> 00:20:03.944
"663 square feet."

00:20:05.451 --> 00:20:09.833
And so all the big plans
had to be scrunched down.

00:20:09.833 --> 00:20:11.532
But it was only gonna be temporary.

00:20:11.532 --> 00:20:15.785
So, "Plan this, but also
plan your future lab,

00:20:15.785 --> 00:20:17.906
"and then figure out how
you're gonna integrate it

00:20:17.906 --> 00:20:19.931
"with the book-repair unit

00:20:19.931 --> 00:20:24.098
"that you actually aren't
gonna be contiguous with."

00:20:25.227 --> 00:20:26.881
But that's what we had in the grant.

00:20:26.881 --> 00:20:28.191
(laughter)

00:20:28.191 --> 00:20:30.585
So we talked with the Mellon Foundation,

00:20:30.585 --> 00:20:31.665
kept them appraised,

00:20:31.665 --> 00:20:35.057
but that meant that I was
juggling a lot of concepts

00:20:35.057 --> 00:20:36.494
and a lot of workflows

00:20:36.494 --> 00:20:40.306
also with the new head of
preservation, Kara McClurken.

00:20:40.306 --> 00:20:42.285
So the idea for the conservation lab

00:20:42.285 --> 00:20:45.545
was build everything
you can do full service,

00:20:45.545 --> 00:20:48.778
just scrunch it up a little bit,

00:20:48.778 --> 00:20:52.126
and make sure that everything
we put in that room

00:20:52.126 --> 00:20:53.832
can move out of that room

00:20:53.832 --> 00:20:56.589
and be implemented in the new facility

00:20:56.589 --> 00:20:59.339
and be grown into a new facility.

00:21:00.182 --> 00:21:02.432
So that's where we started.

00:21:03.577 --> 00:21:06.954
And like I said, 663 square feet.

00:21:06.954 --> 00:21:09.262
And the original plan had been

00:21:09.262 --> 00:21:12.267
all kinds of custom built-in cabinets

00:21:12.267 --> 00:21:16.428
and cubby holes and things
for storage of supplies.

00:21:16.428 --> 00:21:18.466
But there was no way I
was gonna be able to do

00:21:18.466 --> 00:21:20.663
storage of big supplies,

00:21:20.663 --> 00:21:24.830
and the idea of custom
furniture, wooden furniture

00:21:26.146 --> 00:21:27.961
that would then move to a
new facility, I was like,

00:21:27.961 --> 00:21:29.579
"Okay, that's not gonna work."

00:21:29.579 --> 00:21:32.016
And so I changed the concept quite a bit.

00:21:32.016 --> 00:21:32.849
I said,

00:21:32.849 --> 00:21:34.728
"We're gonna go with the university vendor

00:21:34.728 --> 00:21:37.602
"who provides the casework and the benches

00:21:37.602 --> 00:21:40.231
"and the countertops for the science labs,

00:21:40.231 --> 00:21:41.884
"because they will come in,

00:21:41.884 --> 00:21:43.815
"they will do exactly what we want,

00:21:43.815 --> 00:21:45.078
"they will give us a good price,

00:21:45.078 --> 00:21:46.586
"and then when it comes time to move,

00:21:46.586 --> 00:21:50.439
"we can get more casework,
we can get whatever.

00:21:50.439 --> 00:21:52.658
"We can build on the original concept,

00:21:52.658 --> 00:21:56.825
"and they'll still be around
and it'll all work fabulously."

00:21:59.492 --> 00:22:02.575
That was the lab standing at one end,

00:22:04.613 --> 00:22:06.740
and you can see that
the sink and everything

00:22:06.740 --> 00:22:08.987
hasn't been quite attached.

00:22:08.987 --> 00:22:12.971
This is me turning around the other way,

00:22:12.971 --> 00:22:15.002
and ta-da, you see the washing sink,

00:22:15.002 --> 00:22:17.339
the lockers to hang coats and so on.

00:22:17.339 --> 00:22:21.506
And this is before the flat-file
cabinets were installed.

00:22:22.512 --> 00:22:24.463
So while this is all going on,

00:22:24.463 --> 00:22:27.338
I'm designing the
collaborative conservation lab,

00:22:27.338 --> 00:22:30.705
and we're doing donor
packets and brochures,

00:22:30.705 --> 00:22:33.099
and everything was gonna be fabulous.

00:22:33.099 --> 00:22:35.099
And then things changed.

00:22:36.486 --> 00:22:40.877
That investor in New York
turned out to be a fraud,

00:22:40.877 --> 00:22:42.631
and a lot of people who had money

00:22:42.631 --> 00:22:44.906
then didn't have money.

00:22:44.906 --> 00:22:46.656
So it became apparent

00:22:48.065 --> 00:22:50.482
that I was really gonna have
to make a permanent home

00:22:50.482 --> 00:22:52.058
of this facility.

00:22:52.058 --> 00:22:56.225
So seven years in, this is
the facility in working order.

00:22:59.623 --> 00:23:01.133
It is just me,

00:23:01.133 --> 00:23:05.301
or for most of the time
it has been just me

00:23:05.301 --> 00:23:07.390
working in this conservation lab.

00:23:07.390 --> 00:23:12.014
Every summer I would hire a
six-week conservation intern.

00:23:12.014 --> 00:23:14.915
And then the past few years

00:23:14.915 --> 00:23:17.551
I had a graduate intern from NYU.

00:23:17.551 --> 00:23:20.481
So I've been able to spread out,

00:23:20.481 --> 00:23:24.054
which can be really nice if
all the benches are yours.

00:23:24.054 --> 00:23:26.354
(laughs)

00:23:26.354 --> 00:23:29.541
And I can really figure
out how things are, y'know,

00:23:29.541 --> 00:23:31.052
how do things work.

00:23:31.052 --> 00:23:33.949
I can stage treatment really well.

00:23:33.949 --> 00:23:37.876
One of the big drawbacks,
though, is I'm very isolated.

00:23:37.876 --> 00:23:41.840
I am a 15-minute walk from
the main library buildings,

00:23:41.840 --> 00:23:44.745
so we never get donor tours.

00:23:44.745 --> 00:23:47.545
Or we've tried, we have open houses.

00:23:47.545 --> 00:23:49.762
People don't really come,
or it's pouring rain,

00:23:49.762 --> 00:23:51.216
and then nobody really wants to come.

00:23:51.216 --> 00:23:55.803
One open house we had this lady
who trudged through the rain

00:23:55.803 --> 00:23:58.111
with her husband holding
the umbrella over her,

00:23:58.111 --> 00:24:01.001
and she desperately wanted
to see the conservation lab

00:24:01.001 --> 00:24:04.188
because years ago she had been
interested in conservation.

00:24:04.188 --> 00:24:06.328
And her life took another turn,

00:24:06.328 --> 00:24:08.922
and there and then she pulled out a cheque

00:24:08.922 --> 00:24:11.080
and wrote me a cheque for $100.

00:24:11.080 --> 00:24:12.562
Which was really, really sweet,

00:24:12.562 --> 00:24:15.508
but that's the level of
engagement you can get

00:24:15.508 --> 00:24:19.153
when you are not in
easy strolling distance

00:24:19.153 --> 00:24:21.403
from the director's office.

00:24:22.477 --> 00:24:23.901
So that's a big drawback.

00:24:23.901 --> 00:24:26.591
I also spend a lot of time
running back and forth.

00:24:26.591 --> 00:24:28.515
If somebody finds a moldy book

00:24:28.515 --> 00:24:31.432
I get off my chair, hustle on over.

00:24:32.653 --> 00:24:34.820
If things need quick fixes

00:24:36.179 --> 00:24:37.961
in special collections buildings,

00:24:37.961 --> 00:24:41.309
I load up my bag with tools
and methyl cellulose or whatnot

00:24:41.309 --> 00:24:42.522
and I run over, and I'm like,

00:24:42.522 --> 00:24:43.869
"Oh wait, I needed something else,"

00:24:43.869 --> 00:24:46.170
because the person
described it a certain way

00:24:46.170 --> 00:24:48.240
and they didn't use the terminology.

00:24:48.240 --> 00:24:49.917
And again, when you're
the only conservator

00:24:49.917 --> 00:24:51.417
at an institution,

00:24:52.433 --> 00:24:54.298
you're the one-man band.

00:24:54.298 --> 00:24:56.012
You're trying to train the staff

00:24:56.012 --> 00:24:59.685
to understand what you
can do, what you can't do,

00:24:59.685 --> 00:25:02.685
and how to think about conservation.

00:25:04.573 --> 00:25:07.490
So those are some of the drawbacks.

00:25:08.417 --> 00:25:09.481
But that said,

00:25:09.481 --> 00:25:12.971
you can do a lot in 663 square feet,

00:25:12.971 --> 00:25:13.893
although,

00:25:13.893 --> 00:25:15.643
do we have a pointer?

00:25:16.560 --> 00:25:17.633
Oh, no no.

00:25:17.633 --> 00:25:19.096
Come back.

00:25:19.096 --> 00:25:19.929
I was gonna say,

00:25:19.929 --> 00:25:23.962
if you look back to where the peg board is

00:25:23.962 --> 00:25:25.353
over the washing sink,

00:25:25.353 --> 00:25:29.171
to the right there's this
sort of big white square box,

00:25:29.171 --> 00:25:31.671
that's the air-handler system.

00:25:34.587 --> 00:25:37.561
That lab is all we have in that building.

00:25:37.561 --> 00:25:40.561
So when the air-handler system died,

00:25:42.246 --> 00:25:44.233
the facilities guys were like,

00:25:44.233 --> 00:25:45.849
"Well, we've gotta take out the old one

00:25:45.849 --> 00:25:47.538
"and put in a new one,"

00:25:47.538 --> 00:25:48.934
and you're like,

00:25:48.934 --> 00:25:52.069
"Well this is the only
working space I have,

00:25:52.069 --> 00:25:53.317
"I'm in the middle of treatment."

00:25:53.317 --> 00:25:54.888
What do you do?

00:25:54.888 --> 00:25:56.007
You just sort of keep going,

00:25:56.007 --> 00:25:58.482
and the fellas were literally

00:25:58.482 --> 00:26:01.476
chopping up bits of the HVAC
system and pulling it out,

00:26:01.476 --> 00:26:04.248
and then they had to order the new one

00:26:04.248 --> 00:26:05.668
and it was bigger than the old one,

00:26:05.668 --> 00:26:07.367
and so then they had to take down a wall—

00:26:07.367 --> 00:26:08.527
(laughter)

00:26:08.527 --> 00:26:12.611
and make the booth for
the air handler bigger.

00:26:12.611 --> 00:26:14.971
So I did lose a little
bit of square footage.

00:26:14.971 --> 00:26:18.103
Another drawback of this facility

00:26:18.103 --> 00:26:20.746
is that there's no temperature,

00:26:20.746 --> 00:26:22.153
well there's temperature control,

00:26:22.153 --> 00:26:23.768
but no relative humidity control.

00:26:23.768 --> 00:26:24.740
If it rains outside,

00:26:24.740 --> 00:26:26.806
the humidity goes way up.

00:26:26.806 --> 00:26:28.578
And it's no joke, we track it

00:26:28.578 --> 00:26:33.077
and my boss and I are still
advocating for a new space.

00:26:33.077 --> 00:26:35.378
That hasn't happened yet.

00:26:35.378 --> 00:26:36.211
But then again,

00:26:36.211 --> 00:26:38.191
the university is going to be renovating

00:26:38.191 --> 00:26:39.505
the main library building,

00:26:39.505 --> 00:26:42.806
so maybe something will happen there.

00:26:42.806 --> 00:26:44.829
The other tricky thing about this space

00:26:44.829 --> 00:26:48.814
is that people still don't
know what conservation is,

00:26:48.814 --> 00:26:49.784
to a certain degree.

00:26:49.784 --> 00:26:53.278
I go to town meetings
and do presentations.

00:26:53.278 --> 00:26:55.949
I spend a lot of time with
special collections people,

00:26:55.949 --> 00:26:59.193
cultivating them and educating them

00:26:59.193 --> 00:27:01.144
on what conservation can do.

00:27:01.144 --> 00:27:02.614
But then that means
the rest of the library

00:27:02.614 --> 00:27:04.557
doesn't really know.

00:27:04.557 --> 00:27:08.478
So outreach is a constant
effort on my part,

00:27:08.478 --> 00:27:12.162
and a constant effort on my boss's part.

00:27:12.162 --> 00:27:13.722
And then when interns come

00:27:13.722 --> 00:27:18.367
I make them give presentations
and things like that.

00:27:18.367 --> 00:27:19.842
There you have it.

00:27:19.842 --> 00:27:22.163
I think Bobbie's questions for us

00:27:22.163 --> 00:27:24.663
will sort of flesh things out.

00:27:25.625 --> 00:27:27.671
But on to Yale.

00:27:27.671 --> 00:27:29.921
(applause)

00:27:40.675 --> 00:27:41.596
- I have slides,

00:27:41.596 --> 00:27:43.267
but since most of you have seen the spaces

00:27:43.267 --> 00:27:44.688
(laughter)

00:27:44.688 --> 00:27:47.019
I don't feel like I have
to work quite so hard

00:27:47.019 --> 00:27:48.946
to describe the wonderfulness

00:27:48.946 --> 00:27:52.212
(laughter)
that is our new laboratory.

00:27:52.212 --> 00:27:53.228
But as Bobbie said,

00:27:53.228 --> 00:27:55.354
I'm the chief conservator
for the libraries here.

00:27:55.354 --> 00:27:56.967
That means I'm the head of conservation

00:27:56.967 --> 00:27:58.421
and exhibit services.

00:27:58.421 --> 00:28:00.452
And you met many of the fabulous staff

00:28:00.452 --> 00:28:04.363
that I work with to take care
of the library's collections.

00:28:04.363 --> 00:28:05.653
My presentation is entitled

00:28:05.653 --> 00:28:10.112
Conservation Studio to
Conservation Laboratory.

00:28:10.112 --> 00:28:14.279
Some of you may remember or
had seen the space in 1974.

00:28:15.312 --> 00:28:18.380
I actually found the
preservation news announcement

00:28:18.380 --> 00:28:20.978
when Jane Greenfield had turned

00:28:20.978 --> 00:28:23.930
what was a book-binding
studio in the library

00:28:23.930 --> 00:28:27.818
into the library's first
conservation studio.

00:28:27.818 --> 00:28:29.862
It was sort of considered
state-of-the-art,

00:28:29.862 --> 00:28:32.510
all set up, fabulous, good to go.

00:28:32.510 --> 00:28:36.417
We continued to work in
that space for 50 years.

00:28:36.417 --> 00:28:38.800
As you can see in the top corner

00:28:38.800 --> 00:28:40.315
I've got the preservation news.

00:28:40.315 --> 00:28:42.325
Some of you might have
remembered that fume hood.

00:28:42.325 --> 00:28:43.557
Still usin' it.

00:28:43.557 --> 00:28:45.307
It cost $418 in 1974.

00:28:47.004 --> 00:28:49.937
And it does not owe us
a thing at this point.

00:28:49.937 --> 00:28:51.036
(laughter)

00:28:51.036 --> 00:28:55.146
The other pictures are actually
the befores of our space.

00:28:55.146 --> 00:28:57.014
This is 2008.

00:28:57.014 --> 00:28:58.355
So this is when I arrived

00:28:58.355 --> 00:29:00.117
and was hired by the
library to take the post

00:29:00.117 --> 00:29:01.864
as the chief conservator.

00:29:01.864 --> 00:29:04.710
The top is our space on 1NB,

00:29:04.710 --> 00:29:07.620
which is a mezzanine level
in Sterling Memorial Library,

00:29:07.620 --> 00:29:11.168
tucked off stacks, off
publicly-accessible hallways,

00:29:11.168 --> 00:29:14.918
a tiny 1930's-sized
hallway with book stacks.

00:29:16.120 --> 00:29:19.868
So probably about the width of this podium

00:29:19.868 --> 00:29:21.037
to access a single door.

00:29:21.037 --> 00:29:23.180
No secondary egress.

00:29:23.180 --> 00:29:25.651
And an incredibly steep ramp

00:29:25.651 --> 00:29:27.851
that got you down into the sit space

00:29:27.851 --> 00:29:29.632
because it was sunken below.

00:29:29.632 --> 00:29:32.811
The lower picture represents
the fact that at some point

00:29:32.811 --> 00:29:35.938
other parts of the
operation as we expanded,

00:29:35.938 --> 00:29:38.566
the initial space was the whole space,

00:29:38.566 --> 00:29:42.322
so that 1NB, it was
about 1400 square feet.

00:29:42.322 --> 00:29:44.858
General collections, at
some point moved to a space

00:29:44.858 --> 00:29:46.431
in the basement.

00:29:46.431 --> 00:29:49.960
Exhibit functions were also
set up in the basement.

00:29:49.960 --> 00:29:53.335
So then we became a program
on two different floors.

00:29:53.335 --> 00:29:55.303
We weren't really able to do much

00:29:55.303 --> 00:29:57.480
in terms of rearranging
a lot of those spaces.

00:29:57.480 --> 00:30:00.602
None of them were really
purpose-built in the basement.

00:30:00.602 --> 00:30:03.155
And the experience that Eliza described

00:30:03.155 --> 00:30:05.153
for us was sort of a daily occurrence

00:30:05.153 --> 00:30:06.940
because most of our
spaces were built around

00:30:06.940 --> 00:30:11.585
or contained various parts of
the SML's HVAC water systems,

00:30:11.585 --> 00:30:14.597
fire suppression, water sprinkler mains.

00:30:14.597 --> 00:30:16.380
You needed to fix
anything in the building,

00:30:16.380 --> 00:30:19.100
it was somehow in one of our spaces.

00:30:19.100 --> 00:30:21.458
All of the telecommunications wiring

00:30:21.458 --> 00:30:22.794
for the entire building.

00:30:22.794 --> 00:30:25.439
So we had a lot of traffic

00:30:25.439 --> 00:30:27.546
with facilities, outside contractors,

00:30:27.546 --> 00:30:31.139
and others from the library
who needed to come in and out.

00:30:31.139 --> 00:30:33.100
We did not have any way to add space,

00:30:33.100 --> 00:30:35.802
there was absolutely
nowhere to go at this point.

00:30:35.802 --> 00:30:39.000
We could shuffle, we could rearrange.

00:30:39.000 --> 00:30:42.251
In 2010, Bobbie managed
to secure some money

00:30:42.251 --> 00:30:44.345
for us to do a sort of minor renovation

00:30:44.345 --> 00:30:46.431
to the spaces that we were using.

00:30:46.431 --> 00:30:49.695
That involved new lighting
in the basement, new paint.

00:30:49.695 --> 00:30:51.765
We did the most to the lab upstairs

00:30:51.765 --> 00:30:54.261
that had started in '74
as the conservation lab.

00:30:54.261 --> 00:30:57.861
We added a washing sink
instead of a photo sink,

00:30:57.861 --> 00:30:59.150
a very narrow sink that actually,

00:30:59.150 --> 00:31:01.593
according to the pictures
in the '74 article,

00:31:01.593 --> 00:31:03.736
I don't think we ever washed in the sink,

00:31:03.736 --> 00:31:05.677
I think we always washed in trays

00:31:05.677 --> 00:31:07.140
outside of the washing sink,

00:31:07.140 --> 00:31:09.390
because it was an off-the-shelf

00:31:09.390 --> 00:31:13.473
photo-developing sink for
small 8x10 photographs.

00:31:14.692 --> 00:31:15.921
So we added those things,

00:31:15.921 --> 00:31:17.270
and that sort of bought us some time.

00:31:17.270 --> 00:31:18.829
There had been talk about plans

00:31:18.829 --> 00:31:21.042
for a centralized facility

00:31:21.042 --> 00:31:23.709
of various conservation
operations on campus

00:31:23.709 --> 00:31:26.298
on York Street, that York Street building,

00:31:26.298 --> 00:31:28.357
that was the first
project when I got here.

00:31:28.357 --> 00:31:31.361
There was a very large
plan developed for that.

00:31:31.361 --> 00:31:32.977
That did not come to happen.

00:31:32.977 --> 00:31:35.337
Yale purchased the West Campus property

00:31:35.337 --> 00:31:37.024
that's out in Orange,

00:31:37.024 --> 00:31:39.041
and there was talk about that,

00:31:39.041 --> 00:31:41.549
building, from scratch,
a purpose-built facility

00:31:41.549 --> 00:31:42.560
that would house labs

00:31:42.560 --> 00:31:45.314
for all of the conservation
labs on campus.

00:31:45.314 --> 00:31:47.134
Due to the crash in 2008

00:31:47.134 --> 00:31:49.852
there were some changes where
that project was stalled.

00:31:49.852 --> 00:31:51.559
When it re-started,

00:31:51.559 --> 00:31:53.229
basically the scientific arm

00:31:53.229 --> 00:31:54.843
had become incredibly important.

00:31:54.843 --> 00:31:55.831
The idea of building

00:31:55.831 --> 00:31:58.710
a shared analytical and research facility,

00:31:58.710 --> 00:32:00.555
where we could have folks like,

00:32:00.555 --> 00:32:01.815
I see Aniko,

00:32:01.815 --> 00:32:04.405
our director of scientific
research for conservation,

00:32:04.405 --> 00:32:06.777
and those things sort of took precedence,

00:32:06.777 --> 00:32:09.019
and an existing building
needed to be modified.

00:32:09.019 --> 00:32:12.482
Also, the art gallery needed
space for its operations.

00:32:12.482 --> 00:32:15.811
So that plan turned
into a shared facility.

00:32:15.811 --> 00:32:17.395
So there was space at West Campus

00:32:17.395 --> 00:32:19.351
where we could use it
on a temporary basis,

00:32:19.351 --> 00:32:20.767
but there was not sufficient space

00:32:20.767 --> 00:32:23.299
for us to move all of
our operations out there.

00:32:23.299 --> 00:32:24.850
Also, because of the way we work,

00:32:24.850 --> 00:32:26.385
and as people know with libraries,

00:32:26.385 --> 00:32:29.174
we are intimately tied
to the collections here,

00:32:29.174 --> 00:32:32.391
and so, with no other library
presence on West Campus,

00:32:32.391 --> 00:32:35.455
it seemed difficult to move
our day-to-day operations,

00:32:35.455 --> 00:32:37.907
and easier to coordinate
and work at West Campus

00:32:37.907 --> 00:32:38.959
for special projects

00:32:38.959 --> 00:32:40.925
and for anything that involved a kind of

00:32:40.925 --> 00:32:44.008
technical research study of material.

00:32:46.512 --> 00:32:49.892
A lot things started to
happen between 2012 and 2014

00:32:49.892 --> 00:32:52.375
which basically meant that a program

00:32:52.375 --> 00:32:54.991
that was sort of held in
the little bit in stasis

00:32:54.991 --> 00:32:56.856
by the walls that existed,

00:32:56.856 --> 00:32:58.031
the physical walls,

00:32:58.031 --> 00:32:59.939
began to kind of really burst out

00:32:59.939 --> 00:33:03.291
and to respond to current
changes in the field and needs.

00:33:03.291 --> 00:33:07.350
And so in 2012 conservation and
exhibit services was formed.

00:33:07.350 --> 00:33:09.297
General collections had
been a separate unit,

00:33:09.297 --> 00:33:11.376
and so that was brought under,

00:33:11.376 --> 00:33:14.372
so there was the potential
for that kind of crossover

00:33:14.372 --> 00:33:17.299
and cross-pollination in the
ways that we work with things.

00:33:17.299 --> 00:33:19.015
We saw that as an opportunity

00:33:19.015 --> 00:33:20.508
because Yale has collections

00:33:20.508 --> 00:33:23.816
general collections that
really in many places

00:33:23.816 --> 00:33:25.355
are considered special collections.

00:33:25.355 --> 00:33:27.499
And so there are a lot
of transitional material

00:33:27.499 --> 00:33:30.608
that medium-rare concept that
was talked about years ago.

00:33:30.608 --> 00:33:32.800
And so we thought it might
be an interesting thing

00:33:32.800 --> 00:33:34.625
to think about how we
might have to re-tool

00:33:34.625 --> 00:33:37.913
our general collections operation.

00:33:37.913 --> 00:33:41.200
Three workspaces, two different floors,

00:33:41.200 --> 00:33:43.534
three flights apart from one another,

00:33:43.534 --> 00:33:46.975
accessed by very tiny
little winding stairs,

00:33:46.975 --> 00:33:49.502
or a teeny tiny elevator

00:33:49.502 --> 00:33:51.443
in the public stacks hallway.

00:33:51.443 --> 00:33:53.699
As you can imagine, that can be difficult.

00:33:53.699 --> 00:33:55.400
We moved photo-doc out of the main lab,

00:33:55.400 --> 00:33:56.541
but that went down to the basement,

00:33:56.541 --> 00:33:58.574
so that meant every object had to move

00:33:58.574 --> 00:33:59.593
up and down three flights

00:33:59.593 --> 00:34:02.947
for before and after documentation.

00:34:02.947 --> 00:34:05.447
We began to see a real change, though,

00:34:05.447 --> 00:34:07.410
in terms of a lot of
outreach to the campus

00:34:07.410 --> 00:34:09.481
and thinking about how an internal,

00:34:09.481 --> 00:34:10.765
embedded conservation lab

00:34:10.765 --> 00:34:12.928
should really facilitate and add something

00:34:12.928 --> 00:34:15.202
in addition to what a
conservator in private practice

00:34:15.202 --> 00:34:16.277
might bring.

00:34:16.277 --> 00:34:18.348
That was being engaged in teaching,

00:34:18.348 --> 00:34:19.181
and some of the learning

00:34:19.181 --> 00:34:21.236
that's happening on campus with objects.

00:34:21.236 --> 00:34:22.069
So as you can see here,

00:34:22.069 --> 00:34:23.349
we have a number of examples

00:34:23.349 --> 00:34:25.672
of all the different kinds of
things that started happening.

00:34:25.672 --> 00:34:27.668
Analytical testing,
working with scientists,

00:34:27.668 --> 00:34:30.723
working with faculty, working
with outside researchers.

00:34:30.723 --> 00:34:33.252
The lower picture on your left

00:34:33.252 --> 00:34:35.928
is our traveling scriptorium in action.

00:34:35.928 --> 00:34:38.301
So those are students from,
I believe, Barbara Shailor,

00:34:38.301 --> 00:34:39.511
who's with us today,

00:34:39.511 --> 00:34:41.982
her class learning to write,

00:34:41.982 --> 00:34:44.102
pretending to be scribes at a table.

00:34:44.102 --> 00:34:45.970
We did all of that in that tiny space.

00:34:45.970 --> 00:34:48.323
Rearranging shutting down
every part of the operation

00:34:48.323 --> 00:34:49.480
to make that happen.

00:34:49.480 --> 00:34:51.668
And a lot of these things
became very popular.

00:34:51.668 --> 00:34:52.960
As most people know in libraries,

00:34:52.960 --> 00:34:54.853
we see a lot of oversized material.

00:34:54.853 --> 00:34:56.761
So we can't continue to count on things

00:34:56.761 --> 00:34:58.366
being small, rare books.

00:34:58.366 --> 00:34:59.867
As much as we encourage those purchases

00:34:59.867 --> 00:35:00.700
(laughter)

00:35:00.700 --> 00:35:02.008
and I see Ray Clemens, who's like,

00:35:02.008 --> 00:35:03.817
"Little books."

00:35:03.817 --> 00:35:06.598
(mumbles)

00:35:06.598 --> 00:35:07.972
We see Tibetan thangkas,

00:35:07.972 --> 00:35:09.322
we see large, oversized maps.

00:35:09.322 --> 00:35:11.091
We see physical,
three-dimensional objects.

00:35:11.091 --> 00:35:12.534
You may have seen the two Coronelli globes

00:35:12.534 --> 00:35:13.646
that are in the lab right now.

00:35:13.646 --> 00:35:16.022
So the spaces were very
difficult and very challenging

00:35:16.022 --> 00:35:18.231
to re-tool and to change for
the different kinds of things

00:35:18.231 --> 00:35:19.672
we needed to do.

00:35:19.672 --> 00:35:22.829
We also hired Werner Haun who joined us,

00:35:22.829 --> 00:35:25.782
and we have a new team in what we call

00:35:25.782 --> 00:35:27.511
collections, conservation, and housing.

00:35:27.511 --> 00:35:29.062
Lot of new housing projects.

00:35:29.062 --> 00:35:30.195
A lot of need out there

00:35:30.195 --> 00:35:32.102
to house special, unusual objects,

00:35:32.102 --> 00:35:33.356
and just to protect things

00:35:33.356 --> 00:35:35.298
that are going to off-site
shelving facilities.

00:35:35.298 --> 00:35:37.097
So that became a really
full-blown operation

00:35:37.097 --> 00:35:39.316
that sort of transitioned
our general collection

00:35:39.316 --> 00:35:40.182
into something else.

00:35:40.182 --> 00:35:43.054
So folks were really working
together very closely,

00:35:43.054 --> 00:35:45.344
our exhibits program was also growing,

00:35:45.344 --> 00:35:47.179
Kerri Sancombe our
exhibits program manager

00:35:47.179 --> 00:35:49.888
was beginning to think
about student exhibitions

00:35:49.888 --> 00:35:51.448
and working with students

00:35:51.448 --> 00:35:54.636
and needing to bring them into the space.

00:35:54.636 --> 00:35:58.579
So then we get to the Gates
Conservation Laboratory in 2016.

00:35:58.579 --> 00:36:00.343
We are a true hybrid facility,

00:36:00.343 --> 00:36:03.379
and for the first time we
really can bring all of those

00:36:03.379 --> 00:36:05.474
places together that we had in Sterling

00:36:05.474 --> 00:36:08.496
so that the staff who always
worked so well together

00:36:08.496 --> 00:36:11.518
and worked very hard at that collaboration

00:36:11.518 --> 00:36:13.414
and the seamlessness
in terms of what we did

00:36:13.414 --> 00:36:15.745
now have a space that
physically reflects that

00:36:15.745 --> 00:36:17.728
and makes that a lot easier.

00:36:17.728 --> 00:36:19.449
Our design for the lab

00:36:19.449 --> 00:36:23.300
really ended up almost tripling
the existing space we had.

00:36:23.300 --> 00:36:26.703
At our top we were
about 3,000 square feet.

00:36:26.703 --> 00:36:28.183
Not much of that space in Sterling

00:36:28.183 --> 00:36:30.612
was completely useable space.

00:36:30.612 --> 00:36:32.406
I mentioned the ramp.

00:36:32.406 --> 00:36:34.922
We had an opportunity to
create functional areas

00:36:34.922 --> 00:36:35.905
like our wet treatment,

00:36:35.905 --> 00:36:38.590
and photo-doc rooms and
things that were separate

00:36:38.590 --> 00:36:40.500
from the primary main treatment space

00:36:40.500 --> 00:36:42.536
so that people weren't
collapsing or moving

00:36:42.536 --> 00:36:44.502
their personal tables and workspaces

00:36:44.502 --> 00:36:45.624
so that we could accommodate

00:36:45.624 --> 00:36:48.102
what are pretty standard
separate functions

00:36:48.102 --> 00:36:50.665
that are now reserved for
people and they can use them.

00:36:50.665 --> 00:36:52.958
We stuck with a very open
and flexible floor plan,

00:36:52.958 --> 00:36:55.119
and I would say that that is a real credit

00:36:55.119 --> 00:36:58.949
to people like Paula Zyats, our
assistant chief conservator,

00:36:58.949 --> 00:37:01.784
Marie-France Lemay, and all
of the others on our team.

00:37:01.784 --> 00:37:04.201
Karen, and Ansley, and Kerri,

00:37:05.047 --> 00:37:06.957
who we had worked so long and so hard

00:37:06.957 --> 00:37:08.913
in terms of making the other space work,

00:37:08.913 --> 00:37:11.115
that we just kept what
was good about that.

00:37:11.115 --> 00:37:13.498
Which was that we need to
be able to change ourselves

00:37:13.498 --> 00:37:15.776
as we need to respond to what's there.

00:37:15.776 --> 00:37:17.215
And even when you get a lot of space,

00:37:17.215 --> 00:37:19.160
which, we got a lot of space,

00:37:19.160 --> 00:37:21.145
you still need to really think about that

00:37:21.145 --> 00:37:23.172
because no one ever
really has enough space,

00:37:23.172 --> 00:37:24.722
and there's just
different ways to do that.

00:37:24.722 --> 00:37:27.041
So you'll notice that the
built-ins are perimeter,

00:37:27.041 --> 00:37:28.321
not so much in the center.

00:37:28.321 --> 00:37:29.855
We kept our original mobile tables,

00:37:29.855 --> 00:37:31.717
put in tables to match those

00:37:31.717 --> 00:37:35.942
so that we could create
that flexible floor plan.

00:37:35.942 --> 00:37:36.904
And I think the other thing

00:37:36.904 --> 00:37:38.401
that's made a huge difference
for us as I mentioned,

00:37:38.401 --> 00:37:40.267
is critical adjacencies for us.

00:37:40.267 --> 00:37:42.137
We are all together,

00:37:42.137 --> 00:37:44.231
photo-doc is off the main treatment area,

00:37:44.231 --> 00:37:47.006
the collections vault is
right next to photo-doc.

00:37:47.006 --> 00:37:50.151
And I think that helps us to stay in-line

00:37:50.151 --> 00:37:51.956
with what is really good practice,

00:37:51.956 --> 00:37:54.416
because you're not so
tempted so skip a step,

00:37:54.416 --> 00:37:55.249
or to think, you know,

00:37:55.249 --> 00:37:57.318
"I've gotta take this all
the way down three flights,

00:37:57.318 --> 00:37:59.158
"is it really something
I wanna do to this object

00:37:59.158 --> 00:38:00.358
"to take a photograph?"

00:38:00.358 --> 00:38:02.903
So I think we are able
to do all of the things

00:38:02.903 --> 00:38:03.906
that we need to do

00:38:03.906 --> 00:38:06.901
so much more efficiently,
and so much better.

00:38:06.901 --> 00:38:08.233
We do have a little bit of growth space.

00:38:08.233 --> 00:38:10.489
As Beth mentioned, we're looking forward

00:38:10.489 --> 00:38:13.248
to not holding Beinecke classrooms hostage

00:38:13.248 --> 00:38:16.865
while we have consultants
in to survey collections

00:38:16.865 --> 00:38:18.381
or to examine them.

00:38:18.381 --> 00:38:21.058
We can host on-site private conservators

00:38:21.058 --> 00:38:22.366
because we have the space.

00:38:22.366 --> 00:38:25.109
We have never had an intern
in the time that I'm here

00:38:25.109 --> 00:38:25.999
from the graduate programs,

00:38:25.999 --> 00:38:26.832
because honestly

00:38:26.832 --> 00:38:29.007
they would have to sit
on top of someone else.

00:38:29.007 --> 00:38:30.602
Would've had to build a loft

00:38:30.602 --> 00:38:33.035
or some sort of trailer in
the parking lot for them.

00:38:33.035 --> 00:38:34.015
(laughter)

00:38:34.015 --> 00:38:35.960
That is something that
we've been asked every year,

00:38:35.960 --> 00:38:38.584
and so it's nice for the
first time this year to say

00:38:38.584 --> 00:38:40.760
when people call from Buffalo,

00:38:40.760 --> 00:38:43.655
"Yes, we would be happy to consider that."

00:38:43.655 --> 00:38:45.755
I think that the team has
an awful lot to offer,

00:38:45.755 --> 00:38:47.434
and the collections do too.

00:38:47.434 --> 00:38:49.414
The other critical thing
for us that I think works,

00:38:49.414 --> 00:38:51.615
is that the other parts of
the preservation department

00:38:51.615 --> 00:38:53.676
have come with us and
are in the same building.

00:38:53.676 --> 00:38:56.692
And more importantly, with
the Beinecke's location here,

00:38:56.692 --> 00:38:58.913
they're 90% of our
special collections work.

00:38:58.913 --> 00:39:00.107
More importantly,

00:39:00.107 --> 00:39:02.553
it just gives a nice,
healthy library presence

00:39:02.553 --> 00:39:03.528
to the space

00:39:03.528 --> 00:39:06.125
so we don't feel isolated in that way.

00:39:06.125 --> 00:39:08.647
And a lot of our work is
back-and-forth with archivists

00:39:08.647 --> 00:39:09.799
and librarians from there,

00:39:09.799 --> 00:39:12.722
so it makes things really
great and really easy.

00:39:12.722 --> 00:39:14.272
So it's kind of a destination.

00:39:14.272 --> 00:39:17.903
Bacey is with us today,
too, from development,

00:39:17.903 --> 00:39:20.153
and we have had no
shortage of walk-throughs.

00:39:20.153 --> 00:39:21.359
People will come.

00:39:21.359 --> 00:39:23.624
There have been two classes
that have already come through

00:39:23.624 --> 00:39:25.103
in the six months we're here.

00:39:25.103 --> 00:39:27.926
And we have had at least
one development tour

00:39:27.926 --> 00:39:30.950
or walk-through, or special visit

00:39:30.950 --> 00:39:32.475
every month since we've been here,

00:39:32.475 --> 00:39:34.113
and we hope that will continue.

00:39:34.113 --> 00:39:34.977
Thank you.

00:39:34.977 --> 00:39:37.227
(applause)

00:39:41.737 --> 00:39:43.904
- Okay, so, switch this...

00:39:47.307 --> 00:39:48.377
I do have a series of questions.

00:39:48.377 --> 00:39:51.438
Many of the questions have
been addressed in part

00:39:51.438 --> 00:39:53.002
within the talk.

00:39:53.002 --> 00:39:54.463
But it'll give you the opportunity

00:39:54.463 --> 00:39:56.595
to perhaps expand on various areas.

00:39:56.595 --> 00:39:57.555
One of the things,

00:39:57.555 --> 00:39:59.343
I think almost every one of you mentioned

00:39:59.343 --> 00:40:01.423
that your labs are hybrid.

00:40:01.423 --> 00:40:04.214
And perhaps it might be good
to talk a little bit about

00:40:04.214 --> 00:40:07.020
what that is and what's driven that

00:40:07.020 --> 00:40:09.103
sort of way of operating,

00:40:11.298 --> 00:40:13.048
and whether that was,

00:40:13.048 --> 00:40:17.278
is it programmatic, or then also the space

00:40:17.278 --> 00:40:18.479
that it's all in one space.

00:40:18.479 --> 00:40:20.689
Is that programmatic that
you've done the hybrid?

00:40:20.689 --> 00:40:24.560
Or is it something else
that's causing that?

00:40:24.560 --> 00:40:28.792
So, I'll let you figure out
who's gonna answer that first.

00:40:28.792 --> 00:40:32.187
(speaking off mic)

00:40:32.187 --> 00:40:34.911
- I'll start cuz we have the microphone.

00:40:34.911 --> 00:40:35.744
As I mentioned,

00:40:35.744 --> 00:40:38.329
it was a conscious decision on our part,

00:40:38.329 --> 00:40:41.257
because we had had segregated
spaces for so long.

00:40:41.257 --> 00:40:43.819
Obviously kind of
segregated in the extreme.

00:40:43.819 --> 00:40:46.891
But we really did see
that in many respects

00:40:46.891 --> 00:40:48.561
that Yale's general collections operations

00:40:48.561 --> 00:40:49.635
were really changing.

00:40:49.635 --> 00:40:51.932
We still see some of the
minor kinds of repair,

00:40:51.932 --> 00:40:54.163
but we don't see a lot of
straightforward book repair.

00:40:54.163 --> 00:40:56.430
Certainly not the kind that I learned

00:40:56.430 --> 00:40:57.941
when I was first a technician.

00:40:57.941 --> 00:41:01.104
We see much more complicated
and older materials,

00:41:01.104 --> 00:41:02.509
and so this allows us an opportunity

00:41:02.509 --> 00:41:06.294
for the technicians to have
very similar transferable skills

00:41:06.294 --> 00:41:08.911
and for us to form smaller project teams

00:41:08.911 --> 00:41:12.273
that are kind of across all
of those boundaries of skill,

00:41:12.273 --> 00:41:13.946
and it just makes things more flexible

00:41:13.946 --> 00:41:18.471
and we can be better able
to respond to things.

00:41:18.471 --> 00:41:22.199
- As Kara and I have
talked about the future

00:41:22.199 --> 00:41:24.533
of the conservation program at UVA,

00:41:24.533 --> 00:41:28.615
we had one future that we
thought we were planning for

00:41:28.615 --> 00:41:31.823
and with the collaborative
conservation facility

00:41:31.823 --> 00:41:33.297
that was going to be built, we thought

00:41:33.297 --> 00:41:35.802
"Should we plan to move book repair

00:41:35.802 --> 00:41:37.895
"and commercial binding, and everything

00:41:37.895 --> 00:41:39.887
"out of Alderman Library?"

00:41:39.887 --> 00:41:42.208
And ultimately we decided
that we shouldn't,

00:41:42.208 --> 00:41:44.979
because we wanted to
have a physical presence

00:41:44.979 --> 00:41:46.954
in the main library.

00:41:46.954 --> 00:41:48.878
We didn't want people to forget

00:41:48.878 --> 00:41:51.306
what preservation and conservation did,

00:41:51.306 --> 00:41:53.159
and if not enough library people

00:41:53.159 --> 00:41:56.103
were going to move out
to the remote facility,

00:41:56.103 --> 00:41:57.221
whatever it was,

00:41:57.221 --> 00:41:59.663
we thought we would be too isolated,

00:41:59.663 --> 00:42:01.375
people would not know what we did,

00:42:01.375 --> 00:42:04.292
and we would be just some satellite

00:42:05.477 --> 00:42:07.580
where mysterious things happened to books

00:42:07.580 --> 00:42:09.631
and then they came back
in blue plastic totes

00:42:09.631 --> 00:42:11.156
and looked better.

00:42:11.156 --> 00:42:14.791
So we had never planned
to move them all together

00:42:14.791 --> 00:42:18.109
unless we could be part of
a bigger library facility.

00:42:18.109 --> 00:42:20.975
And then with the way things
have worked out at UVA,

00:42:20.975 --> 00:42:22.712
I'm the little satellite

00:42:22.712 --> 00:42:26.392
and then I spend time
coming back and forth.

00:42:26.392 --> 00:42:28.829
I think the hybrid concept,

00:42:28.829 --> 00:42:31.160
it creates a lot of flexibility

00:42:31.160 --> 00:42:32.586
and I think that's really important

00:42:32.586 --> 00:42:36.986
because programs change
and staffing changes,

00:42:36.986 --> 00:42:39.657
and you have opportunities
to hire new people,

00:42:39.657 --> 00:42:43.386
or people move out of your
department for whatever reason.

00:42:43.386 --> 00:42:46.798
So I think having a hybrid
program is really great.

00:42:46.798 --> 00:42:49.413
And I think years ago people were saying,

00:42:49.413 --> 00:42:51.870
"Book repair's gonna go away,"

00:42:51.870 --> 00:42:53.406
and this and that.

00:42:53.406 --> 00:42:55.071
It is gonna be a long, long time

00:42:55.071 --> 00:42:56.785
before book repair goes away.

00:42:56.785 --> 00:42:58.387
So I also think for that reason

00:42:58.387 --> 00:43:01.887
we still need to plan for hybrid programs.

00:43:04.053 --> 00:43:06.150
- At Illinois we are a hybrid lab.

00:43:06.150 --> 00:43:09.817
That was more because
we had no lab to start

00:43:12.151 --> 00:43:13.967
and so we had space for one lab.

00:43:13.967 --> 00:43:16.422
So it, by essential,
had to be a hybrid lab

00:43:16.422 --> 00:43:18.324
because we weren't gonna get
a special collections lab

00:43:18.324 --> 00:43:21.263
any time in the near future.

00:43:21.263 --> 00:43:22.136
What I didn't mention,

00:43:22.136 --> 00:43:23.063
which I think is important,

00:43:23.063 --> 00:43:25.867
is that, similar to some other folks,

00:43:25.867 --> 00:43:30.207
the Harvard-style facility
that our lab is occupied in

00:43:30.207 --> 00:43:31.247
is on the edge of campus.

00:43:31.247 --> 00:43:32.205
So we are similar,

00:43:32.205 --> 00:43:33.829
about a 15-minute walk.

00:43:33.829 --> 00:43:36.678
However, we also have
high-density storage.

00:43:36.678 --> 00:43:39.465
Which, for whatever reason,
it really thrills donors.

00:43:39.465 --> 00:43:42.017
It's that whole Indiana Jones experience.

00:43:42.017 --> 00:43:46.329
So we have no problem getting
donors out to our space.

00:43:46.329 --> 00:43:48.161
But the space we moved out into

00:43:48.161 --> 00:43:50.635
did require us, similar to UVA,

00:43:50.635 --> 00:43:52.281
to separate ourselves from preservation

00:43:52.281 --> 00:43:54.140
and a lot of the adjacencies that we have

00:43:54.140 --> 00:43:56.059
with the preservation program.

00:43:56.059 --> 00:43:58.200
So while the conservation lab is hybrid,

00:43:58.200 --> 00:43:59.941
we are separated from preservation,

00:43:59.941 --> 00:44:03.130
which gives us a different
sort of separation,

00:44:03.130 --> 00:44:04.977
but does bring us back
then to the main library

00:44:04.977 --> 00:44:06.090
a lot more frequently.

00:44:06.090 --> 00:44:07.948
So within our facility,

00:44:07.948 --> 00:44:10.281
we have general book repair,

00:44:11.882 --> 00:44:14.181
the way it was constructed we
would have general book repair

00:44:14.181 --> 00:44:16.715
and then special collections conservation.

00:44:16.715 --> 00:44:19.852
In the 10 years since we
opened the lab, of course,

00:44:19.852 --> 00:44:22.581
similar to what Christine
was implying with Yale,

00:44:22.581 --> 00:44:23.952
we've watched a lot of changes

00:44:23.952 --> 00:44:26.577
in the way we're approaching
our book repair part of it.

00:44:26.577 --> 00:44:28.714
And while, no, book
repair is not going away,

00:44:28.714 --> 00:44:30.101
it is changing.

00:44:30.101 --> 00:44:32.286
And our approaches to it
are definitely changing.

00:44:32.286 --> 00:44:34.207
And with the adjacencies that we have

00:44:34.207 --> 00:44:35.180
with special collections,

00:44:35.180 --> 00:44:37.891
it's allowed us to start
to develop actually third,

00:44:37.891 --> 00:44:41.173
a kind of grey in-between
workflow of medium-rare.

00:44:41.173 --> 00:44:43.548
And to look at all that material
that is not really rare,

00:44:43.548 --> 00:44:44.977
and it's not really general,

00:44:44.977 --> 00:44:47.319
but it's somewhere in the middle.

00:44:47.319 --> 00:44:48.774
And to be able to collaborate

00:44:48.774 --> 00:44:51.130
and to work with people who
have some of the skill sets

00:44:51.130 --> 00:44:52.627
that are more appropriate
in special collections

00:44:52.627 --> 00:44:54.832
even though the material doesn't
sit in special collections

00:44:54.832 --> 00:44:57.689
has allowed us a lot of
programmatic flexibility

00:44:57.689 --> 00:44:59.623
to do what we feel is really appropriate

00:44:59.623 --> 00:45:00.727
for our collections.

00:45:00.727 --> 00:45:03.244
Being, similar to all of us here,

00:45:03.244 --> 00:45:04.997
a large academic library,

00:45:04.997 --> 00:45:06.960
our print's not going anywhere.

00:45:06.960 --> 00:45:09.355
But our print is going to
become more and more important

00:45:09.355 --> 00:45:10.821
as an object over time.

00:45:10.821 --> 00:45:12.506
And so I think the hybrid approach

00:45:12.506 --> 00:45:13.703
is really important for us

00:45:13.703 --> 00:45:15.454
to be able to care for a
massive general collection

00:45:15.454 --> 00:45:17.583
in a way that's important long-term

00:45:17.583 --> 00:45:22.168
not just slapping book
tape on it or whatever.

00:45:22.168 --> 00:45:23.363
- Ditto.

00:45:23.363 --> 00:45:24.608
(laughter)

00:45:24.608 --> 00:45:28.610
- You won't go last next time. (laughs)

00:45:28.610 --> 00:45:30.338
- I don't know what else to add to that,

00:45:30.338 --> 00:45:33.794
other than I think it's
one thing to look at it

00:45:33.794 --> 00:45:35.123
from a collections standpoint,

00:45:35.123 --> 00:45:38.606
but I'll just touch on what it does

00:45:38.606 --> 00:45:40.364
in terms of staff development.

00:45:40.364 --> 00:45:41.593
Having a hybrid lab

00:45:41.593 --> 00:45:45.211
really allows people to participate

00:45:45.211 --> 00:45:47.435
either directly or indirectly

00:45:47.435 --> 00:45:49.913
in skill-building discussions

00:45:49.913 --> 00:45:54.356
that they might not get
to do all of the time.

00:45:54.356 --> 00:45:56.273
Our technicians are all

00:45:57.768 --> 00:45:58.938
trained, to some extent,

00:45:58.938 --> 00:46:01.688
to do general collections work

00:46:01.688 --> 00:46:02.925
and special collections work.

00:46:02.925 --> 00:46:04.676
So if there's an all-lab project

00:46:04.676 --> 00:46:06.122
and we need all hands on deck,

00:46:06.122 --> 00:46:07.940
they have the skills to do that.

00:46:07.940 --> 00:46:10.316
And I think that is really important

00:46:10.316 --> 00:46:13.285
because we hear a lot of rhetoric

00:46:13.285 --> 00:46:16.052
about how the general
collections aren't important,

00:46:16.052 --> 00:46:18.311
they're going away, we don't need them.

00:46:18.311 --> 00:46:19.730
And we know that it's not true,

00:46:19.730 --> 00:46:21.572
and we're actually the
physical manifestation

00:46:21.572 --> 00:46:23.511
of the fact that that's not true.

00:46:23.511 --> 00:46:25.300
And so I think that hybrid space

00:46:25.300 --> 00:46:28.083
really allows us to
show why it's important

00:46:28.083 --> 00:46:31.083
that all of these skills are needed,

00:46:32.323 --> 00:46:35.851
and that the staffing
levels we have are needed

00:46:35.851 --> 00:46:38.601
for the collections that we have.

00:46:39.841 --> 00:46:40.886
- [Pilette] Let me ask one more question,

00:46:40.886 --> 00:46:41.867
then we'll open it up.

00:46:41.867 --> 00:46:42.700
What I wanted to ask is,

00:46:42.700 --> 00:46:44.658
in your planning and design,

00:46:44.658 --> 00:46:46.052
to what extent and in what ways

00:46:46.052 --> 00:46:50.168
were you able to anticipate
the non-treatment things

00:46:50.168 --> 00:46:51.721
that you're having to deal with?

00:46:51.721 --> 00:46:52.827
Beth, you get to go first.

00:46:52.827 --> 00:46:54.646
(laughter)

00:46:54.646 --> 00:46:56.157
Or do you want more time to think?

00:46:56.157 --> 00:46:59.074
- See, that's the hard part, right?

00:47:02.090 --> 00:47:05.201
Doubling our space allowed us to do that.

00:47:05.201 --> 00:47:08.784
Just moving through
our old space was hard.

00:47:11.995 --> 00:47:13.802
Having the space to store things

00:47:13.802 --> 00:47:15.895
or to review things

00:47:15.895 --> 00:47:18.268
or to have curatorial conversations

00:47:18.268 --> 00:47:20.586
is really important.

00:47:20.586 --> 00:47:23.205
And I certainly thought about that.

00:47:23.205 --> 00:47:26.211
Having space to spread out was important.

00:47:26.211 --> 00:47:28.044
The tours have become,

00:47:29.251 --> 00:47:32.392
we're really a shining jewel

00:47:32.392 --> 00:47:34.014
in the crown of this renovation.

00:47:34.014 --> 00:47:37.931
We've been renovating
for almost over 15 years,

00:47:38.874 --> 00:47:40.475
and we're just finished phase three,

00:47:40.475 --> 00:47:41.734
which was special collections.

00:47:41.734 --> 00:47:46.711
And we're still really
benefiting from that limelight.

00:47:46.711 --> 00:47:49.220
And that's been very important.

00:47:49.220 --> 00:47:52.237
And collections conservators
do a lot of things

00:47:52.237 --> 00:47:56.059
that aren't at the bench,
as many of us know,

00:47:56.059 --> 00:47:57.558
and to have the space to meet

00:47:57.558 --> 00:48:00.113
and the space to do
training, and hold workshops,

00:48:00.113 --> 00:48:02.379
both internally or bringing people in

00:48:02.379 --> 00:48:04.962
has been very important for us.

00:48:06.616 --> 00:48:08.241
- For us I think I'll specifically focus

00:48:08.241 --> 00:48:10.957
on our education and outreach program.

00:48:10.957 --> 00:48:12.827
I had to fight really hard

00:48:12.827 --> 00:48:14.924
when we were looking at the
square footage of our lab.

00:48:14.924 --> 00:48:16.732
And when you get the blank slate,

00:48:16.732 --> 00:48:17.860
which all of us have now,

00:48:17.860 --> 00:48:20.329
it's hard to parse out the space

00:48:20.329 --> 00:48:23.857
and recognize, especially for me,

00:48:23.857 --> 00:48:25.638
who was relatively new
to the Illinois program

00:48:25.638 --> 00:48:26.777
and didn't have a lot of experience

00:48:26.777 --> 00:48:28.474
with that their needs were going to be,

00:48:28.474 --> 00:48:31.135
to figure out how much square
footage we needed for each.

00:48:31.135 --> 00:48:32.593
And I had to end up fighting really hard

00:48:32.593 --> 00:48:35.633
for our all-purpose conference classroom,

00:48:35.633 --> 00:48:37.711
what have you space.

00:48:37.711 --> 00:48:40.468
But that was the best
argument I think I made,

00:48:40.468 --> 00:48:43.385
because we bring so many workshops,

00:48:45.335 --> 00:48:50.167
graduate school classes, tours,
advancement opportunities,

00:48:50.167 --> 00:48:51.126
et cetera,

00:48:51.126 --> 00:48:55.699
through that space that it
has paid for itself tenfold.

00:48:55.699 --> 00:48:58.882
And because we're a
land-grant institution,

00:48:58.882 --> 00:49:03.793
because we have that outreach
to the community mentality

00:49:03.793 --> 00:49:05.544
already built in to the library,

00:49:05.544 --> 00:49:07.544
and our library pushes very, very hard

00:49:07.544 --> 00:49:09.126
for all of our librarians to go out

00:49:09.126 --> 00:49:10.567
and do things for the community

00:49:10.567 --> 00:49:13.270
and do things around campus, et cetera,

00:49:13.270 --> 00:49:15.983
that classroom space has been invaluable

00:49:15.983 --> 00:49:17.537
to allow us to do that.

00:49:17.537 --> 00:49:20.199
And I can't imagine being
as successful as we are

00:49:20.199 --> 00:49:22.621
in bringing people in,
and engaging people,

00:49:22.621 --> 00:49:24.560
and making people understand
what conservation is,

00:49:24.560 --> 00:49:26.705
that's not a battle that,
fortunately, I have to fight.

00:49:26.705 --> 00:49:28.221
People get it.

00:49:28.221 --> 00:49:29.809
For whatever reason they do,

00:49:29.809 --> 00:49:31.212
and a lot of that has to do with the fact

00:49:31.212 --> 00:49:32.710
that we're constantly
putting ourselves out there,

00:49:32.710 --> 00:49:36.331
we're constantly finding
reasons to bring people in.

00:49:36.331 --> 00:49:38.004
So we're really closely tied now

00:49:38.004 --> 00:49:40.468
with the graduate school of
library and information science,

00:49:40.468 --> 00:49:41.981
and have a lot of faculty there

00:49:41.981 --> 00:49:45.566
who are better understanding
preservation and conservation,

00:49:45.566 --> 00:49:49.538
and better understanding
the needs of that program

00:49:49.538 --> 00:49:51.908
and how the graduate students
can come get experience there

00:49:51.908 --> 00:49:55.012
and then go out and become
special collections librarians

00:49:55.012 --> 00:49:58.023
who have understanding of conservation.

00:49:58.023 --> 00:50:00.828
And that's been a really
important thing for us,

00:50:00.828 --> 00:50:02.425
that classroom space and building that in

00:50:02.425 --> 00:50:04.342
has been really useful.

00:50:06.387 --> 00:50:07.887
- I think for UVA,

00:50:09.364 --> 00:50:12.598
we've renovated or built
a conservation lab.

00:50:12.598 --> 00:50:15.461
But I think the most
important non-treatment space

00:50:15.461 --> 00:50:18.878
is the way tow bug-killer freezer laughs)

00:50:22.909 --> 00:50:27.347
I think the freezer is in
the preservation office

00:50:27.347 --> 00:50:28.721
with the book repair,

00:50:28.721 --> 00:50:32.054
and having space to spread out the boxes

00:50:33.094 --> 00:50:34.790
and give a quick initial vacuum

00:50:34.790 --> 00:50:37.995
and then load up the freezer
and stage all of that

00:50:37.995 --> 00:50:39.830
is really, really important,

00:50:39.830 --> 00:50:41.943
and I think everyone who
works in a library knows

00:50:41.943 --> 00:50:43.466
that all sorts of things come in

00:50:43.466 --> 00:50:46.876
from all sorts of previous
storage situations,

00:50:46.876 --> 00:50:49.429
and that you need to be
ready to roll with it,

00:50:49.429 --> 00:50:52.924
whether it's bugs or mold or
just lots and lots of dirt

00:50:52.924 --> 00:50:56.068
and mud dauber nests and what have you.

00:50:56.068 --> 00:50:58.963
So I think in terms of
our overall program,

00:50:58.963 --> 00:51:01.957
there's never enough room for that,

00:51:01.957 --> 00:51:04.036
and we're looking at building a

00:51:04.036 --> 00:51:07.490
UVA is looking at building
a second remote-storage

00:51:07.490 --> 00:51:09.027
high-density facility,

00:51:09.027 --> 00:51:11.738
and we are strongly advocating

00:51:11.738 --> 00:51:14.238
for a treatment facility there

00:51:15.117 --> 00:51:16.898
so that we have room to spread out

00:51:16.898 --> 00:51:18.094
and room to stage boxes

00:51:18.094 --> 00:51:21.465
and room to put totes and
plastic bags and so on.

00:51:21.465 --> 00:51:23.561
In terms of the conservation lab,

00:51:23.561 --> 00:51:25.963
I think the lab is so small

00:51:25.963 --> 00:51:28.849
that I had to think about

00:51:28.849 --> 00:51:31.532
treatment in the conservation lab

00:51:31.532 --> 00:51:34.997
but then where could I
stash other workflows.

00:51:34.997 --> 00:51:36.705
So I have the Minter welder,

00:51:36.705 --> 00:51:37.795
as Bill well knows,

00:51:37.795 --> 00:51:41.749
in the stacks of the special
collections building,

00:51:41.749 --> 00:51:46.226
and that's just where it fits
and where the workflow fits.

00:51:46.226 --> 00:51:47.059
Kinda.

00:51:48.557 --> 00:51:50.699
But what has been a real success

00:51:50.699 --> 00:51:54.116
is me giving up control of the photo-doc.

00:51:56.011 --> 00:51:57.279
Originally the plan had been

00:51:57.279 --> 00:52:01.452
to somehow fit a little copy
stand and lights and camera

00:52:01.452 --> 00:52:03.171
in the conservation lab.

00:52:03.171 --> 00:52:04.088
And I said,

00:52:06.229 --> 00:52:08.258
"Space is too much at a premium,"

00:52:08.258 --> 00:52:11.675
and UVA has a huge digitization workflow.

00:52:13.600 --> 00:52:16.156
At this time there are four copy stands

00:52:16.156 --> 00:52:19.906
with very current cameras,
very fancy lights,

00:52:21.545 --> 00:52:23.079
all the computers they need,

00:52:23.079 --> 00:52:25.914
all the student workers they need,

00:52:25.914 --> 00:52:28.138
and they get funded and
everything gets upgraded

00:52:28.138 --> 00:52:29.556
on a regular basis.

00:52:29.556 --> 00:52:31.982
So I partnered with them and said,

00:52:31.982 --> 00:52:35.166
"Hey, since you're
digitizing things anyway,

00:52:35.166 --> 00:52:37.257
"if I need a couple of photos of things

00:52:37.257 --> 00:52:38.322
"can I come in?"

00:52:38.322 --> 00:52:40.373
"Of course, no problem."

00:52:40.373 --> 00:52:42.059
So the benefit to me is

00:52:42.059 --> 00:52:43.746
I don't have to pay for cameras,

00:52:43.746 --> 00:52:46.150
I don't have to know
how the software works,

00:52:46.150 --> 00:52:47.672
I don't have to do quality control,

00:52:47.672 --> 00:52:51.386
or figure out how to
save files the right way.

00:52:51.386 --> 00:52:54.450
There are students who take pity on me

00:52:54.450 --> 00:52:55.736
and do it for the old lady

00:52:55.736 --> 00:52:59.072
who doesn't know computers. (laughs)

00:52:59.072 --> 00:53:00.113
And on the other hand

00:53:00.113 --> 00:53:02.260
they get to see the conservation process

00:53:02.260 --> 00:53:03.532
in before and after

00:53:03.532 --> 00:53:04.910
because if I get things done

00:53:04.910 --> 00:53:07.866
before they leave for the summer

00:53:07.866 --> 00:53:10.386
they get to see that
Jefferson letter come back

00:53:10.386 --> 00:53:11.442
looking much better,

00:53:11.442 --> 00:53:13.398
and that's really exciting for them.

00:53:13.398 --> 00:53:15.173
And I get to spy on their workflows

00:53:15.173 --> 00:53:17.556
and make sure that all the
students are really well-trained

00:53:17.556 --> 00:53:19.353
and handle things very carefully,

00:53:19.353 --> 00:53:22.127
and that the staff in general
is very conscientious.

00:53:22.127 --> 00:53:25.354
So that has been a real
benefit to my program

00:53:25.354 --> 00:53:27.505
that I hadn't anticipated.

00:53:27.505 --> 00:53:31.313
And honestly, I think if you
have a digitization workflow

00:53:31.313 --> 00:53:33.414
that's a great way to go

00:53:33.414 --> 00:53:35.980
because, at least in my world,

00:53:35.980 --> 00:53:39.952
they get all the money, and
all the cameras, and students,

00:53:39.952 --> 00:53:40.785
and so on.

00:53:43.850 --> 00:53:46.724
- I'm tempted to give you
my first answer thought,

00:53:46.724 --> 00:53:47.622
which was,

00:53:47.622 --> 00:53:49.313
"The staff lounge."

00:53:49.313 --> 00:53:50.676
(laughter)

00:53:50.676 --> 00:53:53.774
Which I think for all of
us is pretty eye-opening

00:53:53.774 --> 00:53:54.774
And amazing.

00:53:55.846 --> 00:53:58.167
And I think it does actually serve

00:53:58.167 --> 00:53:59.428
as a great gathering place,

00:53:59.428 --> 00:54:02.480
and there are lots of people
that we run into for breaks.

00:54:02.480 --> 00:54:05.140
There are a lot of informal meetings.

00:54:05.140 --> 00:54:07.193
I had a discussion
about conservation space

00:54:07.193 --> 00:54:10.044
with someone from the
Beinecke a couple of days ago

00:54:10.044 --> 00:54:11.586
which was just over coffee.

00:54:11.586 --> 00:54:13.521
And that kinda thing didn't happen

00:54:13.521 --> 00:54:16.030
when were all so dispersed
because of the nature of,

00:54:16.030 --> 00:54:17.286
Sterling is a beautiful building,

00:54:17.286 --> 00:54:19.317
but Sterling is designed
in a very particular way

00:54:19.317 --> 00:54:21.393
that's maze-like and really doesn't

00:54:21.393 --> 00:54:23.610
have a lot of bring-you-together spaces

00:54:23.610 --> 00:54:26.436
in terms of offices and staff.

00:54:26.436 --> 00:54:30.127
I think, too, for us that
the lab has enough space,

00:54:30.127 --> 00:54:32.682
and certainly the examination
room that we've built in

00:54:32.682 --> 00:54:34.154
is a place where we're thinking

00:54:34.154 --> 00:54:36.087
that's where we host scientists

00:54:36.087 --> 00:54:37.860
who come to do micro-fading testing,

00:54:37.860 --> 00:54:39.681
or we can host a large project

00:54:39.681 --> 00:54:41.810
so that it's a sort of satellite space

00:54:41.810 --> 00:54:43.540
for our West Campus operations.

00:54:43.540 --> 00:54:46.133
It's a place where we could
host a workshop with Soyeon

00:54:46.133 --> 00:54:48.094
from the YCBA.

00:54:48.094 --> 00:54:49.342
And those things are all great.

00:54:49.342 --> 00:54:51.372
I think the other nice
thing about this building

00:54:51.372 --> 00:54:53.193
is that these shared spaces

00:54:53.193 --> 00:54:54.859
are what really make a difference for us.

00:54:54.859 --> 00:54:57.366
There are meeting rooms
for the first time ever

00:54:57.366 --> 00:55:01.213
that are conveniently
located to our workspaces,

00:55:01.213 --> 00:55:03.487
that are pleasant, that
can be scheduled easily.

00:55:03.487 --> 00:55:05.689
This lecture and workshop,

00:55:05.689 --> 00:55:07.113
what we're hosting today,

00:55:07.113 --> 00:55:10.721
is so much easier to do,
and easier to direct people.

00:55:10.721 --> 00:55:14.428
So I think that's been really
great for us to add that.

00:55:14.428 --> 00:55:18.486
And certainly, because we are
really thinking and defining

00:55:18.486 --> 00:55:21.455
conservation lab as also
conservation classroom.

00:55:21.455 --> 00:55:24.283
We really do want students in.

00:55:24.283 --> 00:55:25.907
And so it's great to have that space.

00:55:25.907 --> 00:55:26.936
It's great to have this room

00:55:26.936 --> 00:55:29.719
where we could hold a
lecture, or a small class,

00:55:29.719 --> 00:55:31.635
or partner with someone.

00:55:31.635 --> 00:55:34.379
The intake rooms that are
across the hall from us

00:55:34.379 --> 00:55:35.797
from the Beinecke,

00:55:35.797 --> 00:55:38.573
yesterday I looked at a book
that was delivered to us

00:55:38.573 --> 00:55:41.676
that a curator is
considering for acquisition.

00:55:41.676 --> 00:55:43.527
So that was easy to run across the hall

00:55:43.527 --> 00:55:44.816
and take a look at that.

00:55:44.816 --> 00:55:47.303
And so I think the synergies of our work,

00:55:47.303 --> 00:55:48.279
and those relationships,

00:55:48.279 --> 00:55:50.547
and the way that this new
building space has been designed

00:55:50.547 --> 00:55:52.775
is an added plus in addition to

00:55:52.775 --> 00:55:56.275
an unbelievably fabulous laboratory space.

00:55:58.244 --> 00:56:00.262
- [Pilette] Okay so I'm
gonna open it to questions.

00:56:00.262 --> 00:56:01.631
Do people have questions or do they wanna.

00:56:01.631 --> 00:56:03.031
Ray, go ahead.

00:56:03.031 --> 00:56:07.198
Anybody who wants to pass a
question in to the center aisle,

00:56:08.068 --> 00:56:09.698
Marie will pick up.

00:56:09.698 --> 00:56:11.179
But Ray, why don't you kick us off?

00:56:11.179 --> 00:56:12.445
- [Ray] Okay, so while people are writing.

00:56:12.445 --> 00:56:14.280
It seems like for a lot of you

00:56:14.280 --> 00:56:15.923
getting this nice new space

00:56:15.923 --> 00:56:19.389
was at the cost of moving
away from the reading room.

00:56:19.389 --> 00:56:20.827
And I was just curious,

00:56:20.827 --> 00:56:22.797
obviously there are pros and cons,

00:56:22.797 --> 00:56:25.208
but have there been significant cons

00:56:25.208 --> 00:56:26.041
being further away

00:56:26.041 --> 00:56:29.290
from where you might be needed
for emergency sort of things

00:56:29.290 --> 00:56:30.244
or consultation.

00:56:30.244 --> 00:56:32.600
Or often there's a lot of
times people come for books

00:56:32.600 --> 00:56:35.250
and they're in conservation
and not knowing where the are.

00:56:35.250 --> 00:56:36.675
So I was wondering if some of you

00:56:36.675 --> 00:56:40.855
or all of you could address
that sort of trade-off.

00:56:40.855 --> 00:56:41.921
- [Pilette] Jen, you were nodding.

00:56:41.921 --> 00:56:43.840
- Sure, I was nodding.

00:56:43.840 --> 00:56:45.590
Yes, I would say that

00:56:47.075 --> 00:56:48.163
I can think,

00:56:48.163 --> 00:56:50.841
I mean our new space is
awesome and that's a huge pro.

00:56:50.841 --> 00:56:52.565
It was the available space that we had,

00:56:52.565 --> 00:56:55.503
but given the choice, I
would have had that new lab

00:56:55.503 --> 00:56:58.110
in the main library,
absolutely positively.

00:56:58.110 --> 00:57:00.868
There is no benefit to
not being near your stuff

00:57:00.868 --> 00:57:02.669
and not being near the curators.

00:57:02.669 --> 00:57:04.855
We're fortunate that, similar to Eliza,

00:57:04.855 --> 00:57:07.209
at least preservation
is in the main building.

00:57:07.209 --> 00:57:09.849
So when there is a crisis or something

00:57:09.849 --> 00:57:14.534
there are trained staff to
be able to respond to that.

00:57:14.534 --> 00:57:16.951
However, we've had an ongoing

00:57:19.492 --> 00:57:21.045
challenge with shipping.

00:57:21.045 --> 00:57:25.551
And the security of shipping
as well as the security of,

00:57:25.551 --> 00:57:28.998
moving, not the actual transport,

00:57:28.998 --> 00:57:30.241
which is its own issue,

00:57:30.241 --> 00:57:31.074
but then the

00:57:31.074 --> 00:57:31.907
"Did you get the stuff?"

00:57:31.907 --> 00:57:32.740
"Yes, we got the stuff."

00:57:32.740 --> 00:57:33.991
"How are you proving
that you got the stuff?"

00:57:33.991 --> 00:57:35.577
"Well we go this stuff and this stuff,"

00:57:35.577 --> 00:57:36.951
and going back to the
rare book room and saying

00:57:36.951 --> 00:57:37.784
"We got it."

00:57:37.784 --> 00:57:40.270
And then them accusing
when we send stuff back

00:57:40.270 --> 00:57:41.103
of them then saying,

00:57:41.103 --> 00:57:42.093
"You never sent the stuff."

00:57:42.093 --> 00:57:43.258
"Yeah, we sent the stuff."

00:57:43.258 --> 00:57:44.849
And it would be a lot easier

00:57:44.849 --> 00:57:45.809
if we were all in the same building

00:57:45.809 --> 00:57:47.142
and just carrying it down, and saying

00:57:47.142 --> 00:57:48.464
"Here it is."

00:57:48.464 --> 00:57:49.989
So that has been a huge challenge.

00:57:49.989 --> 00:57:51.937
I think with 10 years of practice

00:57:51.937 --> 00:57:54.406
we found a very workable
solution for that,

00:57:54.406 --> 00:57:56.072
but unfortunately that
does frequently involve

00:57:56.072 --> 00:57:57.135
people's personal cars,

00:57:57.135 --> 00:57:59.307
which is not the way I'd like to do it.

00:57:59.307 --> 00:58:01.406
But the fact is you can't always
coordinate shipping to come

00:58:01.406 --> 00:58:04.286
and do last pickup at your building

00:58:04.286 --> 00:58:06.055
and first pickup at the main library

00:58:06.055 --> 00:58:08.931
and security-wise for special collections

00:58:08.931 --> 00:58:10.176
you can't do anything but that.

00:58:10.176 --> 00:58:12.499
You're not gonna leave
it sitting in the truck.

00:58:12.499 --> 00:58:14.047
So that has been a real challenge.

00:58:14.047 --> 00:58:18.075
And I think the biggest problem, honestly.

00:58:18.075 --> 00:58:19.792
We haven't had that many
instances of someone

00:58:19.792 --> 00:58:23.775
trying to find something
that's over in the lab.

00:58:23.775 --> 00:58:26.223
Usually when that has happened

00:58:26.223 --> 00:58:28.438
we can at least account for
it and get it over there

00:58:28.438 --> 00:58:30.442
or whatever, so that really
hasn't been an issue.

00:58:30.442 --> 00:58:32.798
But for us, the biggest challenge

00:58:32.798 --> 00:58:34.894
has just been the transport of materials.

00:58:34.894 --> 00:58:37.638
But then it's the
water-cooler conversations.

00:58:37.638 --> 00:58:39.026
That's why I'm so jealous of here,

00:58:39.026 --> 00:58:41.318
is you're with the
special collections staff.

00:58:41.318 --> 00:58:44.611
So you can have those
over-coffee random conversations

00:58:44.611 --> 00:58:47.301
that, being separated from
all of our special collections

00:58:47.301 --> 00:58:48.895
we simply don't have.

00:58:48.895 --> 00:58:52.133
And there's a lot of really
beneficial conversations

00:58:52.133 --> 00:58:54.638
that aren't scheduled
at meetings that happen

00:58:54.638 --> 00:58:57.114
that we now have to schedule time.

00:58:57.114 --> 00:58:58.856
And we do encourage
all of our conservators

00:58:58.856 --> 00:59:01.667
to basically schedule
office hours, more or less.

00:59:01.667 --> 00:59:02.574
Now, the problem with us

00:59:02.574 --> 00:59:04.885
is that we have five
distributed special collections.

00:59:04.885 --> 00:59:06.985
So it's not like we can
just load, go one place,

00:59:06.985 --> 00:59:08.032
and be there for an hour.

00:59:08.032 --> 00:59:10.338
We have to rotate through all
of our special collections

00:59:10.338 --> 00:59:12.757
to be able to make sure
that we're that available.

00:59:12.757 --> 00:59:14.988
And they're not even all
in the main building.

00:59:14.988 --> 00:59:16.900
But we do try very hard

00:59:16.900 --> 00:59:18.468
to get out to all of those spaces.

00:59:18.468 --> 00:59:21.949
I would say the one benefit
that we have with that

00:59:21.949 --> 00:59:24.123
is that because I'm the
head of both preservation

00:59:24.123 --> 00:59:25.038
and conservation,

00:59:25.038 --> 00:59:27.146
I have offices in both buildings.

00:59:27.146 --> 00:59:28.928
So if they have a conservation question,

00:59:28.928 --> 00:59:30.265
they can often at least come to me

00:59:30.265 --> 00:59:31.463
and I'll be around.

00:59:31.463 --> 00:59:33.103
But that still isn't the
same as talking to the people

00:59:33.103 --> 00:59:34.432
who are actually treating their objects,

00:59:34.432 --> 00:59:35.765
which is not me.

00:59:39.751 --> 00:59:42.288
- I would echo a lot
of what Jen has to say,

00:59:42.288 --> 00:59:45.582
and then, I do have office
hours every Thursday.

00:59:45.582 --> 00:59:48.106
I'm in special collections 9-12,

00:59:48.106 --> 00:59:50.776
and I'm doing re-housing projects,

00:59:50.776 --> 00:59:53.229
or I'm looking over books
that have been acquired

00:59:53.229 --> 00:59:56.006
and adding them to my to-do list,

00:59:56.006 --> 00:59:58.304
and being a general pest

00:59:58.304 --> 01:00:00.871
so that people know I'm there to help out.

01:00:00.871 --> 01:00:04.288
And then I have meetings there and so on.

01:00:05.647 --> 01:00:07.400
And then when I have graduate students

01:00:07.400 --> 01:00:10.387
they have office hours as well,

01:00:10.387 --> 01:00:12.477
and I think wherever you wind up working,

01:00:12.477 --> 01:00:16.315
you do have to learn how
to stage your projects.

01:00:16.315 --> 01:00:18.819
So I think that's a really
valuable experience.

01:00:18.819 --> 01:00:21.370
It can be tricky, though.

01:00:21.370 --> 01:00:22.203
Unlike Jen,

01:00:22.203 --> 01:00:25.812
I have a van driver that I
can just call up and say,

01:00:25.812 --> 01:00:28.556
"Okay, we need to schedule a trip,"

01:00:28.556 --> 01:00:32.287
and they think it's exciting
to come to the conservation lab

01:00:32.287 --> 01:00:34.037
so they make it work.

01:00:34.999 --> 01:00:39.496
And I just then have to get
someone from special collections

01:00:39.496 --> 01:00:42.720
to ride shotgun and then stage the

01:00:42.720 --> 01:00:45.754
roll it through the tunnels
to the loading dock.

01:00:45.754 --> 01:00:48.859
But then, it happens infrequently enough

01:00:48.859 --> 01:00:50.511
that everybody gets on board.

01:00:50.511 --> 01:00:53.552
Then again, I have to work
really hard to stage treatements

01:00:53.552 --> 01:00:54.385
and think,

01:00:54.385 --> 01:00:56.219
"Okay, I'm gonna take in,"

01:00:56.219 --> 01:00:57.805
like, one of my pictures,

01:00:57.805 --> 01:01:00.044
there was a giant salter on the bench.

01:01:00.044 --> 01:01:02.080
"Okay, I'm gonna take in the two salters,

01:01:02.080 --> 01:01:03.456
"and that also means I have room

01:01:03.456 --> 01:01:06.166
"for maybe two letters and that's it.

01:01:06.166 --> 01:01:07.452
"And then when I finish the salters

01:01:07.452 --> 01:01:09.825
"I can maybe take in some
architectural drawings,"

01:01:09.825 --> 01:01:11.288
So I have to think really carefully

01:01:11.288 --> 01:01:13.732
about what I can accommodate in my space

01:01:13.732 --> 01:01:18.185
and get back to the reading
room in a timely fashion.

01:01:18.185 --> 01:01:19.711
Things can't live out in my lab

01:01:19.711 --> 01:01:21.517
because people need them back there

01:01:21.517 --> 01:01:23.849
and they can't just pop
in and check a page.

01:01:23.849 --> 01:01:26.270
Every now and then I've had a request,

01:01:26.270 --> 01:01:27.469
"Well, a student has a project,"

01:01:27.469 --> 01:01:28.302
and I'm like,

01:01:28.302 --> 01:01:31.161
"Well okay, what date
range does she need it?"

01:01:31.161 --> 01:01:33.242
and then I'll take pictures and send them.

01:01:33.242 --> 01:01:34.409
But that's it.

01:01:36.260 --> 01:01:37.667
- [Pilette] Is there a question?

01:01:37.667 --> 01:01:38.500
Bob?

01:01:39.533 --> 01:01:42.366
- [Bob] Yeah, a civilian question.

01:01:43.236 --> 01:01:46.362
Actually two questions,
and one's very finite,

01:01:46.362 --> 01:01:48.089
one could be very expansive.

01:01:48.089 --> 01:01:49.173
The very finite is,

01:01:49.173 --> 01:01:52.296
what's the difference between
conservation and preservation?

01:01:52.296 --> 01:01:56.469
More expansive is, I see
from your presntations

01:01:56.469 --> 01:01:59.077
a field that sounds like it's very new,

01:01:59.077 --> 01:02:02.748
if 1974 is the earliest date we have.

01:02:02.748 --> 01:02:06.915
And you presumably represent
the best of conservation

01:02:08.778 --> 01:02:11.028
(laughter)

01:02:15.500 --> 01:02:16.516
Is this a new field?

01:02:16.516 --> 01:02:18.683
What was done before 1974?

01:02:20.043 --> 01:02:22.705
- So I guess I'll take that.

01:02:22.705 --> 01:02:23.538
I've got a mic.

01:02:23.538 --> 01:02:25.788
(laughter)

01:02:27.503 --> 01:02:29.090
The difference between
preservation and conservation,

01:02:29.090 --> 01:02:31.035
in the library field,
library and archives,

01:02:31.035 --> 01:02:33.542
is preservation is the umbrella operation.

01:02:33.542 --> 01:02:35.198
In other words, it takes
care of all sorts of things.

01:02:35.198 --> 01:02:38.574
It can deal with the HVAC,
the environmental conditions.

01:02:38.574 --> 01:02:42.154
It can deal with commercial
binding, reformatting,

01:02:42.154 --> 01:02:43.509
brittle paper, that sort of thing,

01:02:43.509 --> 01:02:48.355
as well as conservation
and individual treatments.

01:02:48.355 --> 01:02:51.914
So we're using preservation
in a very broad way.

01:02:51.914 --> 01:02:54.166
Conservation is more narrow.

01:02:54.166 --> 01:02:56.799
And the other question as
to the age of the field

01:02:56.799 --> 01:02:58.632
depends upon what you,

01:02:59.932 --> 01:03:01.122
I guess we would,

01:03:01.122 --> 01:03:01.955
where do we—

01:03:01.955 --> 01:03:02.788
(guests speaking off mic)

01:03:02.788 --> 01:03:04.033
Do we start with the Florence flood?

01:03:04.033 --> 01:03:05.830
Yeah, we start with the Florence flood.

01:03:05.830 --> 01:03:07.997
1966 when the Arno flooded

01:03:10.369 --> 01:03:13.179
there was a huge response

01:03:13.179 --> 01:03:16.338
because of the number of
libraries and archives in Florence

01:03:16.338 --> 01:03:19.083
that were totally inundated with water.

01:03:19.083 --> 01:03:21.782
So people from all over the world

01:03:21.782 --> 01:03:24.782
came to help rescue these materials.

01:03:26.159 --> 01:03:27.789
And because of this,

01:03:27.789 --> 01:03:31.217
there were experts, binders, scientists,

01:03:31.217 --> 01:03:33.041
who for the first time

01:03:33.041 --> 01:03:36.360
suddenly came together
and had to work together

01:03:36.360 --> 01:03:40.371
rather than in their own
little studio or whatever.

01:03:40.371 --> 01:03:42.788
And sharing what worked best.

01:03:44.303 --> 01:03:48.033
At that point, the
field sort of marks that

01:03:48.033 --> 01:03:51.200
as being the beginning of conservation

01:03:52.551 --> 01:03:54.557
and library preservation

01:03:54.557 --> 01:03:58.693
as the start of the
profession, as it were.

01:03:58.693 --> 01:03:59.942
There's always been book binders,

01:03:59.942 --> 01:04:02.442
there's always been restorers.

01:04:03.407 --> 01:04:04.935
But this was the first time that people

01:04:04.935 --> 01:04:07.128
really came together and
started sharing the knowledge

01:04:07.128 --> 01:04:10.806
and deciding we need to do
something in a more formal way.

01:04:10.806 --> 01:04:13.786
In Florence they started training programs

01:04:13.786 --> 01:04:16.722
that really have been
working on for 50 years

01:04:16.722 --> 01:04:19.783
the conservation of
materials that were rescued

01:04:19.783 --> 01:04:22.632
as a result of that flood.

01:04:22.632 --> 01:04:24.567
- If I could just add
to that a little bit.

01:04:24.567 --> 01:04:28.463
Before the Florence flood there
was a man named Mr. Barrow

01:04:28.463 --> 01:04:29.963
who was laminating

01:04:31.719 --> 01:04:33.529
our founding father documents—

01:04:33.529 --> 01:04:34.860
- [Teper] With the best
of intentions. (laughs)

01:04:34.860 --> 01:04:36.326
- Yes, yes.

01:04:36.326 --> 01:04:37.958
And the reason I know this

01:04:37.958 --> 01:04:41.685
is because at the University
of Virginia we were very,

01:04:41.685 --> 01:04:44.983
Mr. Barrow was at the Library
of Virginia in Richmond,

01:04:44.983 --> 01:04:46.845
Charlotteville's just up the road.

01:04:46.845 --> 01:04:51.333
We had oodles and oodles
of Jefferson, Washington,

01:04:51.333 --> 01:04:54.501
Madison documents that
were being laminated.

01:04:54.501 --> 01:04:58.826
Now, Mr. Barrow started out
doing research on brittle paper,

01:04:58.826 --> 01:05:02.338
and he was very worried
about machine-made paper

01:05:02.338 --> 01:05:03.520
from ground wood pulp

01:05:03.520 --> 01:05:06.168
and he was a scientist and did research,

01:05:06.168 --> 01:05:07.001
and was like,

01:05:07.001 --> 01:05:08.775
"Gee all this acidic paper's very brittle,

01:05:08.775 --> 01:05:10.069
"what are we gonna do?

01:05:10.069 --> 01:05:11.860
"We can't reverse the acids,

01:05:11.860 --> 01:05:14.084
"so let's laminate everything,"

01:05:14.084 --> 01:05:18.251
like those driver's licenses
we got back in the day.

01:05:21.310 --> 01:05:24.072
And since doing something to brittle paper

01:05:24.072 --> 01:05:25.572
is worth doing well,

01:05:25.572 --> 01:05:27.969
doing something to
founding fathers documents

01:05:27.969 --> 01:05:29.677
is even better.

01:05:29.677 --> 01:05:33.763
So that effort started up
before the Florence flood,

01:05:33.763 --> 01:05:37.063
but then people started noticing

01:05:37.063 --> 01:05:39.639
that plastic doesn't age very well,

01:05:39.639 --> 01:05:41.562
and it started warping.

01:05:41.562 --> 01:05:44.424
And so that is another sort
of thread of conservation,

01:05:44.424 --> 01:05:46.884
where people were making the best efforts,

01:05:46.884 --> 01:05:48.624
and they were doing it scientifically,

01:05:48.624 --> 01:05:51.537
and they were taking notes
and doing research and so on,

01:05:51.537 --> 01:05:54.442
and then there were
unintended consequences,

01:05:54.442 --> 01:05:56.859
and I think Barrow lamination

01:05:57.884 --> 01:06:01.601
is one area where conservation then says,

01:06:01.601 --> 01:06:04.757
"Wow, one of our core concepts,

01:06:04.757 --> 01:06:06.642
"is we have to be reversible.

01:06:06.642 --> 01:06:09.570
"We have to be very
thorough in our research

01:06:09.570 --> 01:06:11.173
"and very thorough in our approach,

01:06:11.173 --> 01:06:13.145
"because with the best of intentions

01:06:13.145 --> 01:06:17.013
"you can take all these
very important documents

01:06:17.013 --> 01:06:18.914
"and cover them in plastic

01:06:18.914 --> 01:06:21.093
"and turn them in to
little laminated placemats,

01:06:21.093 --> 01:06:23.926
"and that's not what we wanna do."

01:06:24.770 --> 01:06:25.711
So that's another—

01:06:25.711 --> 01:06:27.358
- Right, yeah.

01:06:27.358 --> 01:06:29.886
The Library of Congress and the,

01:06:29.886 --> 01:06:33.275
well, Library of Congress got
started after Florence flood.

01:06:33.275 --> 01:06:35.561
The national archives was the one

01:06:35.561 --> 01:06:38.146
that was sort of leading that.

01:06:38.146 --> 01:06:39.677
What has happened as a result of that

01:06:39.677 --> 01:06:42.909
was they've also started undoing
a lot of the Barrow stuff.

01:06:42.909 --> 01:06:43.771
Nancy?

01:06:43.771 --> 01:06:45.328
- [Nancy] I would just
say another landmark

01:06:45.328 --> 01:06:49.171
would be the Cooperstown
program in the 1950s

01:06:49.171 --> 01:06:53.338
so that conservation of
painting and art began before

01:06:54.921 --> 01:06:56.291
books and paper
- Yes, right.

01:06:56.291 --> 01:06:57.170
Well that started,

01:06:57.170 --> 01:06:59.167
Harvard had a training program.

01:06:59.167 --> 01:07:01.489
Gettens started there, yeah.

01:07:01.489 --> 01:07:03.247
So there's a long history.

01:07:03.247 --> 01:07:07.847
The museum programs and the
field of museum conservation

01:07:07.847 --> 01:07:10.036
is much older than the library.

01:07:10.036 --> 01:07:12.379
But, I mean, you go
back to the Renaissance

01:07:12.379 --> 01:07:14.301
there were restorers.

01:07:14.301 --> 01:07:16.511
There was always somebody
who could clean a painting

01:07:16.511 --> 01:07:17.476
or fix book.

01:07:17.476 --> 01:07:21.636
- [Man] In-house
department for conserving—

01:07:21.636 --> 01:07:24.576
- For library conservation is '70s

01:07:24.576 --> 01:07:26.130
with the Library of Congress.

01:07:26.130 --> 01:07:29.692
I think they're pretty much the first one.

01:07:29.692 --> 01:07:30.525
I'm sorry?

01:07:30.525 --> 01:07:31.756
- [Woman] Used to have binderies before.

01:07:31.756 --> 01:07:33.406
- Binderies, yes.

01:07:33.406 --> 01:07:35.533
In fact, at Columbia University

01:07:35.533 --> 01:07:38.533
what was the conservation department

01:07:39.887 --> 01:07:41.921
was, in fact, a bindery.

01:07:41.921 --> 01:07:45.796
And they had big machines
that they took out.

01:07:45.796 --> 01:07:47.449
Same thing at the Newberry Library.

01:07:47.449 --> 01:07:49.137
There was a commercial bindery

01:07:49.137 --> 01:07:52.903
that really repaired the
books, bound the books.

01:07:52.903 --> 01:07:54.560
So it was in the '70s and '80s where

01:07:54.560 --> 01:07:57.310
start seeing about how important,

01:07:58.625 --> 01:07:59.893
the material culture.

01:07:59.893 --> 01:08:01.781
What we call material culture.

01:08:01.781 --> 01:08:02.831
And how important it is

01:08:02.831 --> 01:08:05.998
to save that information for scholars.

01:08:07.140 --> 01:08:09.841
So we can continue the
conversation over wine.

01:08:09.841 --> 01:08:12.473
(laughter)

01:08:12.473 --> 01:08:14.023
We have a reception—

01:08:14.023 --> 01:08:15.026
(person speaks off mic)

01:08:15.026 --> 01:08:15.859
Oh, one more question, yes.

01:08:15.859 --> 01:08:18.362
- [Person In Audience]
It's related to the process

01:08:18.362 --> 01:08:20.023
of developing the design.

01:08:20.023 --> 01:08:23.168
You talked about the
professionals involved

01:08:23.168 --> 01:08:25.918
(speaks off mic)

01:08:28.500 --> 01:08:30.888
- I think for our program I can say,

01:08:30.888 --> 01:08:33.991
because it was very
recent, I remember it well,

01:08:33.991 --> 01:08:35.389
it's a combination.

01:08:35.389 --> 01:08:37.430
So there was an architectural firm

01:08:37.430 --> 01:08:38.964
who was responsible, Apicella and Bunton,

01:08:38.964 --> 01:08:41.237
for the design of this facility.

01:08:41.237 --> 01:08:43.760
As well as we had a
consultant, Sam Anderson,

01:08:43.760 --> 01:08:44.593
who came in and did

01:08:44.593 --> 01:08:45.436
(phone rings)
our initial space planning

01:08:45.436 --> 01:08:48.093
for the preservation department in general

01:08:48.093 --> 01:08:49.378
to help confirm our numbers.

01:08:49.378 --> 01:08:52.559
They also stayed on to do
some technical cosulting

01:08:52.559 --> 01:08:56.236
on some of the very unique
things to conservation labs,

01:08:56.236 --> 01:08:58.013
because our architectural firm

01:08:58.013 --> 01:09:00.362
had not done a conservation lab before.

01:09:00.362 --> 01:09:01.853
One of the nice things is

01:09:01.853 --> 01:09:04.302
I feel like we had a very nice partnership

01:09:04.302 --> 01:09:06.388
with Apicella and Bunton
about conservation.

01:09:06.388 --> 01:09:09.262
They were very interested
in what we wanted to do

01:09:09.262 --> 01:09:12.512
and very open to our helping to design.

01:09:13.369 --> 01:09:16.560
We could even send little
tracing paper drawings

01:09:16.560 --> 01:09:17.736
of different kinds of layout ideas

01:09:17.736 --> 01:09:19.581
and they were not the
least bit offended by that,

01:09:19.581 --> 01:09:22.213
and able to turn it into something else

01:09:22.213 --> 01:09:25.106
and to help us refine the details.

01:09:25.106 --> 01:09:26.010
So I would say yeah,

01:09:26.010 --> 01:09:29.179
it's a trio of a very
specialized architect,

01:09:29.179 --> 01:09:31.350
our primary architectural firm,

01:09:31.350 --> 01:09:34.301
and then the staff
themselves in conservation.

01:09:34.301 --> 01:09:37.532
And also the myriad of
people that we've talked to

01:09:37.532 --> 01:09:39.946
throughout the field who
have done labs before us.

01:09:39.946 --> 01:09:41.450
All of us had contacts,

01:09:41.450 --> 01:09:43.371
all of us had some previous experience.

01:09:43.371 --> 01:09:45.241
You know what you like, you
know what you don't like,

01:09:45.241 --> 01:09:47.321
and then you know you can
call Jen or Eliza to say,

01:09:47.321 --> 01:09:49.630
"How did you make that work?

01:09:49.630 --> 01:09:51.791
"What do I tell them?"

01:09:51.791 --> 01:09:53.864
- [Pilette] I would say Jen
would be the next good one,

01:09:53.864 --> 01:09:55.390
since you edited the book.

01:09:55.390 --> 01:09:57.580
(laughter)

01:09:57.580 --> 01:09:59.780
- Speaking from my experience,

01:09:59.780 --> 01:10:04.331
I am jealous of being able
to bring in Sam Anderson

01:10:04.331 --> 01:10:05.482
as a consultant.

01:10:05.482 --> 01:10:07.433
Most of us don't have that kind of funding

01:10:07.433 --> 01:10:08.732
available to us.

01:10:08.732 --> 01:10:10.673
Or at least we didn't.

01:10:10.673 --> 01:10:11.506
But I was fortunate,

01:10:11.506 --> 01:10:13.189
so we had an architectural
firm, obviously,

01:10:13.189 --> 01:10:14.497
who was involved in the design.

01:10:14.497 --> 01:10:16.558
But they had no idea
what conservation was,

01:10:16.558 --> 01:10:19.068
so there was a really big
learning curve for them

01:10:19.068 --> 01:10:20.056
in trying to grapple,

01:10:20.056 --> 01:10:22.056
"What is this space,
what does it need to do?

01:10:22.056 --> 01:10:22.935
"Why do you need a dirty room?

01:10:22.935 --> 01:10:23.803
"What's a dirty room?

01:10:23.803 --> 01:10:24.658
"Why do you need a fume hood?

01:10:24.658 --> 01:10:25.491
"Is this a lab?

01:10:25.491 --> 01:10:26.858
"Do we need to build a chemistry lab?"

01:10:26.858 --> 01:10:27.829
"No, it's not a chemistry lab,

01:10:27.829 --> 01:10:30.809
"but it's like a chemistry lab, sorta."

01:10:30.809 --> 01:10:32.611
So there was a lot of education there.

01:10:32.611 --> 01:10:35.811
But where I really benefited was

01:10:35.811 --> 01:10:38.202
our head of facilities within our library

01:10:38.202 --> 01:10:39.899
who was obviously very integrated in this,

01:10:39.899 --> 01:10:41.864
because all construction
goes through his office,

01:10:41.864 --> 01:10:44.793
was an architect previous to that.

01:10:44.793 --> 01:10:48.136
So he served as a really great translator,

01:10:48.136 --> 01:10:49.118
because he understood the library,

01:10:49.118 --> 01:10:50.134
he understood the needs.

01:10:50.134 --> 01:10:52.072
I actually have a fantastic
relationship with him,

01:10:52.072 --> 01:10:53.560
he really gets preservation,

01:10:53.560 --> 01:10:56.110
and understood what conservation was

01:10:56.110 --> 01:10:57.344
and what we were trying to do.

01:10:57.344 --> 01:10:58.993
And so I could talk at him,

01:10:58.993 --> 01:11:00.091
and he'd understand what I was saying.

01:11:00.091 --> 01:11:01.183
And then he could talk to the architect,

01:11:01.183 --> 01:11:03.214
and tell the architect what
the architect wanted to know,

01:11:03.214 --> 01:11:04.930
which I had a really hard time doing.

01:11:04.930 --> 01:11:06.454
So that relationship

01:11:06.454 --> 01:11:09.383
was actually really,
really beneficial to us.

01:11:09.383 --> 01:11:12.335
- [Pilette] Beth or
Eliza, anything to add?

01:11:12.335 --> 01:11:14.704
- I would just say it's really important

01:11:14.704 --> 01:11:17.812
for staff to take the time
to really think through

01:11:17.812 --> 01:11:19.920
what they want in terms of workflows

01:11:19.920 --> 01:11:22.329
and workflow adjacency.

01:11:22.329 --> 01:11:25.252
And to really work out their arguments

01:11:25.252 --> 01:11:28.004
as for why things need
to be the way they are.

01:11:28.004 --> 01:11:31.474
I renovated and then designed a new lab

01:11:31.474 --> 01:11:33.494
at the Smithsonian libraries,

01:11:33.494 --> 01:11:37.688
and the fact that all the staff chimed in

01:11:37.688 --> 01:11:41.282
and did mock-ups after
mock-up after mock-up

01:11:41.282 --> 01:11:42.704
of the workspace

01:11:42.704 --> 01:11:44.350
really made a huge difference.

01:11:44.350 --> 01:11:46.585
And we were part of, at the Smithsonian,

01:11:46.585 --> 01:11:48.501
a very big, big project.

01:11:48.501 --> 01:11:50.511
We were just this little cog,

01:11:50.511 --> 01:11:51.844
and so we would,

01:11:53.265 --> 01:11:55.197
they produce what is it?

01:11:55.197 --> 01:11:58.727
35% drawings, 55% drawings, 75% drawings,

01:11:58.727 --> 01:12:02.185
and every time a big set
of drawings comes out,

01:12:02.185 --> 01:12:05.523
we were given a half
hour with those drawings

01:12:05.523 --> 01:12:07.302
to go through everything

01:12:07.302 --> 01:12:10.663
and double-check that all the
people with the CAD programs

01:12:10.663 --> 01:12:12.352
were doing everything correctly.

01:12:12.352 --> 01:12:15.021
So we would just run into the room,

01:12:15.021 --> 01:12:16.110
they would literally open the door,

01:12:16.110 --> 01:12:16.943
we'd run in.

01:12:16.943 --> 01:12:17.776
It was like,

01:12:17.776 --> 01:12:18.609
"You take the plumbing,

01:12:18.609 --> 01:12:19.442
"I'll take the electric,"

01:12:19.442 --> 01:12:20.502
da da da da.

01:12:20.502 --> 01:12:22.388
And we would just go
through it and say like,

01:12:22.388 --> 01:12:25.137
"Okay, do they still have
the fume trunks in there?

01:12:25.137 --> 01:12:27.349
"Do they still have the
exhaust fans on the roof?"

01:12:27.349 --> 01:12:29.275
And then things would drop out.

01:12:29.275 --> 01:12:30.795
And you would just put a little Post-It,

01:12:30.795 --> 01:12:31.628
and the architect would say,

01:12:31.628 --> 01:12:32.461
"Great, thank you.

01:12:32.461 --> 01:12:33.294
"Okay thanks.

01:12:33.294 --> 01:12:34.127
"Okay thanks."

01:12:34.127 --> 01:12:35.669
Cuz they're not checking the work

01:12:35.669 --> 01:12:37.930
the way you check the work.

01:12:37.930 --> 01:12:39.012
They don't realize

01:12:39.012 --> 01:12:41.932
that they've dropped a fan off the ceiling

01:12:41.932 --> 01:12:43.679
which would, down the road,

01:12:43.679 --> 01:12:45.512
mean a huge price tag.

01:12:48.420 --> 01:12:49.253
And maybe the,

01:12:49.253 --> 01:12:51.235
"Do you really need that fume trunk?

01:12:51.235 --> 01:12:54.177
"Cuz we're gonna have to
punch a hole in the building."

01:12:54.177 --> 01:12:56.490
So I think taking that time

01:12:56.490 --> 01:12:59.809
and really being sure
of what your needs are

01:12:59.809 --> 01:13:02.406
and what the things that
you need to check out

01:13:02.406 --> 01:13:03.557
and the drawings are,

01:13:03.557 --> 01:13:06.362
that makes a huge difference.

01:13:06.362 --> 01:13:09.932
And then, in terms of my work at UVA,

01:13:09.932 --> 01:13:13.733
when I was planning the
lab that didn't happen,

01:13:13.733 --> 01:13:15.499
again being very passionate

01:13:15.499 --> 01:13:16.988
and being very articulate

01:13:16.988 --> 01:13:21.822
about why the things need
to be the way they are.

01:13:21.822 --> 01:13:24.183
We had a collaborative
conservation facility,

01:13:24.183 --> 01:13:27.010
that's what we kept calling it.

01:13:27.010 --> 01:13:28.371
It was goinna be a paintings lab,

01:13:28.371 --> 01:13:31.996
an art-on-paper lab, a
book conservation lab,

01:13:31.996 --> 01:13:33.804
and then the office of the architect

01:13:33.804 --> 01:13:37.230
wanted this huge sort of
pieces-of-buildings lab.

01:13:37.230 --> 01:13:39.504
So very different operations,

01:13:39.504 --> 01:13:41.089
but the architect kept saying,

01:13:41.089 --> 01:13:42.848
"We don't want walls.

01:13:42.848 --> 01:13:45.437
"We want everybody to collaborate.

01:13:45.437 --> 01:13:46.896
"You've gotta be able to collaborate."

01:13:46.896 --> 01:13:47.729
And it's like,

01:13:47.729 --> 01:13:50.003
"We can walk through doors if need be.

01:13:50.003 --> 01:13:53.728
"How 'bout giving us a
shared resource room,

01:13:53.728 --> 01:13:56.477
"or library with the
kitchen and the lunch room

01:13:56.477 --> 01:13:57.310
"and what have you.

01:13:57.310 --> 01:13:58.997
"So-and-so's gonna be making dust

01:13:58.997 --> 01:14:02.261
"because that's what architecture does.

01:14:02.261 --> 01:14:04.791
"Paintings people aren't gonna want that

01:14:04.791 --> 01:14:05.678
"anywhere near.

01:14:05.678 --> 01:14:07.312
"And they're gonna need different lighting

01:14:07.312 --> 01:14:10.529
"and different paint on the
walls than the book people,"

01:14:10.529 --> 01:14:12.049
and so on.

01:14:12.049 --> 01:14:13.999
So you have to keep making those arguments

01:14:13.999 --> 01:14:18.324
because it's really
important that you win them.

01:14:18.324 --> 01:14:19.846
- I know I'm standing in the way of wine,

01:14:19.846 --> 01:14:21.109
(laughter)
but I will just very quickly

01:14:21.109 --> 01:14:24.760
say that it is also sometimes helpful

01:14:24.760 --> 01:14:28.472
to bring in somebody from the outside

01:14:28.472 --> 01:14:30.153
who can say things.

01:14:30.153 --> 01:14:32.426
And I've served that function.

01:14:32.426 --> 01:14:35.516
I've been a consultant on
lab renovation projects

01:14:35.516 --> 01:14:36.656
for other institutions.

01:14:36.656 --> 01:14:39.788
And I'm saying the exact
same thing their staff is,

01:14:39.788 --> 01:14:41.027
but because I don't work there,

01:14:41.027 --> 01:14:44.277
there's a different level of listening.

01:14:46.259 --> 01:14:49.426
And we don't all charge $50,000 a day.

01:14:51.613 --> 01:14:54.442
So if you need an extra nudge

01:14:54.442 --> 01:14:56.310
to say why it's important

01:14:56.310 --> 01:14:57.782
there's people out there

01:14:57.782 --> 01:15:00.116
who will give you a day or two or three

01:15:00.116 --> 01:15:01.336
or a week of your time

01:15:01.336 --> 01:15:03.328
that's affordable, and
it's just another voice

01:15:03.328 --> 01:15:07.495
to add to the din of voices
that they hear every day.

01:15:09.705 --> 01:15:10.538
- Alright.

01:15:10.538 --> 01:15:11.371
Thank you very much,

01:15:11.371 --> 01:15:13.215
and the wine is out the door to the right.

01:15:13.215 --> 01:15:15.465
(applause)

01:15:17.138 --> 01:15:20.638
(gentle xylophone jingle)

